, (np.float64(2635.1074531249997), np.float64(776.2804947916666))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 87, 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'parent_id': '6b79fb31318d166ce2c6e9a1ea1e0ab4', 'category': 'NarrativeText', 'element_id': '4ce97ef86ce75a9dcb8715a73819ad7c'}, page_content='In the OTA Routing Domain, each multicast packet is identified by an HDLC address as it is sent from the PP to the satellite router. There are 128 multicast HDLC addresses available in each beam. However, this does not limit the number of multicast streams in a beam to 128. The multicast HDLC address is assigned to the Multicast Subscriber Service Plan Profile (SSPP), which will typically cover a range of multicast IP streams.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'detection_class_prob': 0.9421328902244568, 'is_extracted': 'true', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(568.75), np.float64(1118.9888281249998)), (np.float64(568.75), np.float64(1353.5252864583333)), (np.float64(2640.561279296875), np.float64(1353.5252864583333)), (np.float64(2640.561279296875), np.float64(1118.9888281249998))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 87, 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'parent_id': '6b79fb31318d166ce2c6e9a1ea1e0ab4', 'category': 'NarrativeText', 'element_id': '150059937d976bb40d60ee3356130626'}, page_content='Implementing multicast for streaming applications is advantageous using broadcast satellites, but can be challenging in spot beam satellite networks. Velocity addresses these challenges by sending a multicast stream only on iNets containing a satellite router that has requested that stream.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'detection_class_prob': 0.943487823009491, 'is_extracted': 'true', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(568.75), np.float64(1399.7179947916666)), (np.float64(568.75), np.float64(1572.275286458333)), (np.float64(2608.310791015625), np.float64(1572.275286458333)), (np.float64(2608.310791015625), np.float64(1399.7179947916666))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 87, 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'parent_id': '6b79fb31318d166ce2c6e9a1ea1e0ab4', 'category': 'NarrativeText', 'element_id': '183dda7df19ed995b532ac6d388d549a'}, page_content='The multicast stream follows a terminal as it switches beams: the PP begins pro-actively sending the stream to the iNet to which the terminal is moving, before the terminal actually makes the switch. See Multicast Subscriber Service Plans on page 140.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'detection_class_prob': 0.8839869499206543, 'is_extracted': 'true', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(300.5321350097656), np.float64(1666.2916666666667)), (np.float64(300.5321350097656), np.float64(1753.7916666666667)), (np.float64(1886.810302734375), np.float64(1753.7916666666667)), (np.float64(1886.810302734375), np.float64(1666.2916666666667))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 87, 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'parent_id': '6b79fb31318d166ce2c6e9a1ea1e0ab4', 'category': 'Title', 'element_id': '13d426863a3bb0802d2bc6b5d3acd8c1'}, page_content='2.4 The Velocity Backbone Network'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'detection_class_prob': 0.9515678286552429, 'is_extracted': 'true', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(564.165771484375), np.float64(1797.1138281249998)), (np.float64(564.165771484375), np.float64(2279.5669531249996)), (np.float64(2649.915771484375), np.float64(2279.5669531249996)), (np.float64(2649.915771484375), np.float64(1797.1138281249998))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 87, 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'parent_id': '13d426863a3bb0802d2bc6b5d3acd8c1', 'category': 'NarrativeText', 'element_id': 'dbbd1f8c66ea8b836cdeb2ec56b6f756'}, page_content='The backbone network is a global or regional network that provides connectivity between SASs, NOCs, CDCs, RF Gateways, and multiple POPs, peering sites, or other locations that support high bandwidth connectivity to subscriber networks. The backbone network typically consists of IP routers interconnected via high-speed terrestrial links as required to support the expected traffic. The backbone network may also consist of leased lines or IP VPNs procured from a terrestrial network operator. Regardless, the backbone network’s design is determined by the satellite operator, who ensures the backbone network provides sufficient capacity and resiliency. No iDirect-supplied equipment is included in the backbone network.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'detection_class_prob': 0.9414627552032471, 'is_extracted': 'true', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(568.75), np.float64(2325.759661458333)), (np.float64(568.75), np.float64(2560.2961197916666)), (np.float64(2624.144775390625), np.float64(2560.2961197916666)), (np.float64(2624.144775390625), np.float64(2325.759661458333))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 87, 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'parent_id': '13d426863a3bb0802d2bc6b5d3acd8c1', 'category': 'NarrativeText', 'element_id': '3567d9f3b1390361c209205efa9d9803'}, page_content='Backbone networks are divided logically into core routing functionality and edge routing functionality. Typically, Velocity sites (SASs, NOCs, CDCs, RF Gateways, and EAPs) connect to the backbone network via a redundant pair of edge routers and standard IP protocols. Only standard protocols and interfaces are required on the edge routers.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'detection_class_prob': 0.9417529106140137, 'is_extracted': 'true', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(568.5576171875), np.float64(2606.4888281249996)), (np.float64(568.5576171875), np.float64(2841.025286458333)), (np.float64(2630.8782864583336), np.float64(2841.025286458333)), (np.float64(2630.8782864583336), np.float64(2606.4888281249996))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 87, 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_u