elSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 110, 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'parent_id': '26092149153ce1f178879e75928251a6', 'category': 'Title', 'element_id': 'd441a0bac5006114519be46a05eb2e92'}, page_content='2.16.3 Virtual Routing and Forwarding (VRF)-Lite'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'detection_class_prob': 0.9436672925949097, 'is_extracted': 'true', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(560.9075317382812), np.float64(506.4888281249999)), (np.float64(560.9075317382812), np.float64(866.2479858398438)), (np.float64(2611.785888671875), np.float64(866.2479858398438)), (np.float64(2611.785888671875), np.float64(506.4888281249999))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 110, 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'parent_id': 'd441a0bac5006114519be46a05eb2e92', 'category': 'NarrativeText', 'element_id': '84991a104858006f8cd2796d52f06753'}, page_content='The Velocity system supports VRF-Lite. VRF-lite is a particular way of using the VRF technology. A VRF is a standalone routing table with its own set of interfaces, its own CEF instance and its own rules about populating and sharing its contents. Only interfaces associated with a particular VRF can communicate with each other (provided that by normal routing rules, a packet entering one of this set of interfaces has its destination reachable via another interface in this set, and this destination is properly recorded in the VRF).'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'detection_class_prob': 0.9427651166915894, 'is_extracted': 'true', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(564.6021118164062), np.float64(911.1763281249998)), (np.float64(564.6021118164062), np.float64(1207.691953125)), (np.float64(2593.9921875), np.float64(1207.691953125)), (np.float64(2593.9921875), np.float64(911.1763281249998))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 110, 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'parent_id': 'd441a0bac5006114519be46a05eb2e92', 'category': 'NarrativeText', 'element_id': '13af386e04f929d685792cbc04ef1aca'}, page_content='Interfaces in different VRFs in general cannot talk to each other. There are, however, some specific exceptions. VRFs are to routers what VLANs are to switches—they both allow you to create multiple virtual devices on top of the physical device. With VLANs, you virtualize a single switch into multiple virtual switches. With VRFs, you virtualize a single router into multiple virtual routers. VRFs, used as described here, is considered VRF-Lite.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'detection_class_prob': 0.9383332133293152, 'is_extracted': 'true', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(565.9521484375), np.float64(1257.5304947916666)), (np.float64(565.9521484375), np.float64(1430.087786458333)), (np.float64(2586.7272447916666), np.float64(1430.087786458333)), (np.float64(2586.7272447916666), np.float64(1257.5304947916666))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 110, 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'parent_id': 'd441a0bac5006114519be46a05eb2e92', 'category': 'NarrativeText', 'element_id': 'd9ab5a7a3b619a3e3a467c2344a0ddf1'}, page_content='In a way similar to switches and VLANs, you group a set of interfaces into a VRF and thereby limit their mutual visibility to them alone, isolating them from all other interfaces on the router.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'detection_class_prob': 0.9043244123458862, 'is_extracted': 'true', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(840.7654418945312), np.float64(1520.0304947916663)), (np.float64(840.7654418945312), np.float64(1754.5669531249998)), (np.float64(2564.7428697916666), np.float64(1754.5669531249998)), (np.float64(2564.7428697916666), np.float64(1520.0304947916663))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 110, 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'category': 'NarrativeText', 'element_id': '35eb57d7cbb40600fa80c3f6776cff0a'}, page_content='NOTE: The Velocity system employs what is referred to as VRF-Lite. It is considered a full VRF when another technology such as MPLS or LISP is added to communicate with other routers having similar VRFs while allowing to carry all traffic via a single interface and being able to tell the packets apart.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'detection_class_prob': 0.9084485769271851, 'is_extracted': 'true', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(313.54166666666663), np.float64(1888.6875)), (np.float64(313.54166666666663), np.f