f', 'detection_class_prob': 0.917690098285675, 'is_extracted': 'true', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(564.8387451171875), np.float64(400.7596614583332)), (np.float64(564.8387451171875), np.float64(511.3377864583335)), (np.float64(2485.628286458333), np.float64(511.3377864583335)), (np.float64(2485.628286458333), np.float64(400.7596614583332))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 135, 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'parent_id': '062380c5db9e87eec6704f02d88fad44', 'category': 'NarrativeText', 'element_id': '162b8a886e2f5ab6cc37f4ba0cad895c'}, page_content='The SI tables are periodically broadcast on the DVB-S2 outbound, with the re-broadcast interval nominally sets to every 5 seconds.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'detection_class_prob': 0.9140699505805969, 'is_extracted': 'true', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(568.75), np.float64(599.3846614583333)), (np.float64(568.75), np.float64(662.5669531249997)), (np.float64(1908.5612835654165), np.float64(662.5669531249997)), (np.float64(1908.5612835654165), np.float64(599.3846614583333))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 135, 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'parent_id': '062380c5db9e87eec6704f02d88fad44', 'category': 'Title', 'element_id': '940af05c3cc2ba31c9db3e07369dfe70'}, page_content='3.5.1.2 Network Clock Recovery Timestamp'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'detection_class_prob': 0.9497073888778687, 'is_extracted': 'true', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(568.75), np.float64(707.0096614583332)), (np.float64(568.75), np.float64(1189.4627864583335)), (np.float64(2629.431884765625), np.float64(1189.4627864583335)), (np.float64(2629.431884765625), np.float64(707.0096614583332))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 135, 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'parent_id': '940af05c3cc2ba31c9db3e07369dfe70', 'category': 'NarrativeText', 'element_id': '5b8e72e72597c0e8e24fd31e14b10c9b'}, page_content='The iDirect system uses a variant of the Network Clock Reference (NCR) scheme as described in the DVB-RCS standard. It is modified to work with LEGS encapsulation instead of MPEG Transport Streams. A high-accuracy or precision timing source, such as a global positioning system (GPS) receiver, is commonly used as a timing reference for the NCR module inside the PP stack. All Tx line cards, receive (Rx) line cards, stand-by line cards in a SAS, and all satellite terminals from all networks that belong to the same SAS are synchronized with the NCR module clock. Furthermore, dynamic MODCOD switching between inroutes in iDirect’s A- TDMA system requires precise synchronization between Rx line cards and satellite terminals.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'detection_class_prob': 0.9534136056900024, 'is_extracted': 'true', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(566.338134765625), np.float64(1235.41552734375)), (np.float64(566.338134765625), np.float64(1842.8292236328125)), (np.float64(2626.058349609375), np.float64(1842.8292236328125)), (np.float64(2626.058349609375), np.float64(1235.41552734375))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 135, 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'parent_id': '940af05c3cc2ba31c9db3e07369dfe70', 'category': 'NarrativeText', 'element_id': '9ce71d6f0743a8e39bd9db9be7c0ee3c'}, page_content='In the iDirect system, NCR packets are always transmitted on the lowest MODCOD supported by the network. The message is transmitted 32 times per second to indicate the current NCR count at the SAS. The NCR timestamp messages define a precise Rx NCR time at the terminal, referenced to the incoming L-band signal. However, in order to line up the transmit times of different satellite terminals, each satellite terminal must have a unique Tx NCR time. Events transmitted from different terminals at the Tx NCR time for each satellite terminal should arrive at the satellite simultaneously. All transmissions are done relative to the Tx NCR time. In particular, the NCR time in the SCT is the Tx NCR time. In addition, the UCP messages are corrections applied to the Tx NCR time. Typically, the Tx NCR time is managed by keeping track of an offset to the Rx NCR time.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'detection_class_prob': 0.8993353247642517, 'is_extracted': 'true', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(568.75), np.float64(1930.1138281249998)), (np.float64(568.75), np.float64(1993.2961197916666)), (np.float64(1311.893310546875), np.float64(1993.2961197916666)), (np.float64(1311.893310546875), np.float64(1930.1138281249998))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number