d': '13b00e9835bb2d807d2b1f071a905340'}, page_content='A Velocity network may be designed to contain a pair of SASs for a satellite — for example, a primary SAS and a backup SAS. The SAS that is currently providing service for the satellite is said to be “active” and the other said to be “standby” or “redundant”.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'detection_class_prob': 0.9426472783088684, 'is_extracted': 'true', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(568.75), np.float64(1576.7681884765625)), (np.float64(568.75), np.float64(1875.9830322265625)), (np.float64(2632.0449531249997), np.float64(1875.9830322265625)), (np.float64(2632.0449531249997), np.float64(1576.7681884765625))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 225, 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'parent_id': 'b1020d0e8bfd42f449d101f8516bccdc', 'category': 'NarrativeText', 'element_id': 'e79b574f0172c8564cc19f671ef8b606'}, page_content='The backup SAS may be physically identical to the primary SAS to ensure seamless system operation in the event of a switchover for an extended period, in which case the designation “primary” and “backup” is effectively arbitrary. Alternatively, the backup SAS may be sized to support less than the full capacity of the primary SAS. In that case, during a SAS switchover the iNets will be established in priority order, and lower priority iNets will become inactive.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'detection_class_prob': 0.5334492325782776, 'is_extracted': 'true', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(1280.9056396484375), np.float64(3085.337646484375)), 