he internet or customer private network through the backbone network to the velocity system, and then to the local network and subnet, with laptops connected to the local network. The satellite dishes represent the communication links between the satellite and the respective networks.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(568.75), np.float64(787.4999999999999)), (np.float64(568.75), np.float64(2038.0208333333333)), (np.float64(2625.0), np.float64(2038.0208333333333)), (np.float64(2625.0), np.float64(787.4999999999999))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 47, 'image_path': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-47-5.jpg', 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'category': 'Image', 'element_id': '3133fa4a1eccf0261ec7b7f41d97583f', 'source_image': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-47-5.jpg'}, page_content='[IMAGE DESCRIPTION]\nSource File: /Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-47-5.jpg\nThe image is a detailed diagram explaining various components of a satellite communication system, specifically focusing on the concepts of channels, carriers, beams, and iNets. Here is a breakdown of the elements present in the image:\n\n1. **Central Diagram**:\n   - The central part of the image shows a satellite dish (represented by a white dish with a black support structure) emitting signals in multiple directions.\n   - The satellite dish is connected to a feeder link, which is represented by a series of lines extending from the satellite dish to a ground station (represented by another satellite dish).\n\n2. **Text Descriptions**:\n   - **Channel**: A channel is described as a bidirectional bandwidth in both the "feeder link" and the "user link". It has an uplink and downlink frequency range for both the user link and the feeder link.\n   - **Multiple channels can be put in the same beam**: This indicates that multiple channels can be transmitted simultaneously within the same beam.\n   - **A channel can contain multiple carriers**: This means that a single channel can carry multiple modulated signals.\n   - **Beam**: A beam is described as a physical footprint on the ground. It may be steerable, meaning it can be directed to different areas on the ground.\n\n3. **Frequency and Power Charts**:\n   - Two frequency-power charts are shown to the right of the central diagram.\n   - The first chart shows a single frequency-power relationship, with a single peak indicating a single carrier.\n   - The second chart shows multiple frequency-power relationships, with multiple peaks indicating multiple carriers within the same frequency range.\n\n4. **Carrier and iNet Definitions**:\n   - **Carrier**: A carrier is defined as an actual modulated signal, for example, a DVB-S2 outbound.\n   - **iNet**: An iNet is defined as a DVB-S2 outbound plus an inroute group.\n\n5. **Ground Station Diagram**:\n   - To the left of the central diagram, there is a smaller diagram showing a ground station with a satellite dish. This represents the receiving end of the communication system.\n\n6. **Beam Diagram**:\n   - Below the central diagram, there is a diagram showing multiple beams (represented by ellipses) emanating from the satellite dish. This illustrates the coverage area of the satellite.\n\nThe image uses a combination of text, diagrams, and charts to explain the technical aspects of satellite communication, focusing on the concepts of channels, carriers, beams, and iNets.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/r