l (EAP) system, which is part of a larger network infrastructure. Here is a detailed description of the image:\n\n1. **Left Side: External Access Portal (EAP)**\n   - **Virtual Network Operator 1**: This section shows two end-user customers, labeled as "End User Customer," connected to the EAP.\n   - **Virtual Network Operator 2**: This section also shows two end-user customers, labeled as "End User Customer," connected to the EAP.\n   - **EAP**: The External Access Portal is the central component of this section. It is depicted as a web browser icon with a lock symbol, indicating security. Arrows point from the end-user customers to the EAP, suggesting that these users access the EAP through their web browsers.\n   - **Pulse EAP**: Below the EAP icon, the text "Pulse EAP" is written, indicating that the EAP is an instance of Pulse.\n\n2. **Right Side: Firewall and NOC Operations**\n   - **Firewall**: The firewall is divided into two sections: "DMZ" and "Proxy." The DMZ (Demilitarized Zone) is a network area that sits between the external network and the internal network. The Proxy section is on the right side of the firewall.\n   - **NOC Operations**: This section shows a group of people, labeled as "Pulse Core," indicating the operations team responsible for the Pulse system. Arrows point from the DMZ and Proxy sections to the Pulse Core, suggesting that these sections are part of the Pulse system and are monitored by the NOC operations team.\n   - **Text Below the Diagram**: \n     - "EAP is a Pulse instance" - This text explains that the EAP is an instance of the Pulse system.\n     - "Login determines access" - This text indicates that user login credentials are used to determine access to the system.\n     - "Pulse Core is protected" - This text suggests that the Pulse Core is a secure and protected part of the system.\n\nThe diagram uses icons and text to illustrate the flow of access from end-users to the Pulse system, highlighting the security measures and 