t side of the diagram.\n   - Service Area A2 is outlined in red and is located on the right side of Service Area A1.\n\n2. **Service Area Group B**:\n   - Contains two service areas: Service Area B1 and Service Area B2.\n   - Service Area B1 is outlined in blue and is located on the bottom left side of the diagram.\n   - Service Area B2 is outlined in blue and is located on the top right side of the diagram.\n\nThe diagram uses arrows to point to each service area, indicating their respective groups. The text labels for the service areas are clearly written in red and blue, corresponding to their respective groups. The diagram is simple and uses geometric shapes to represent the service areas.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(561.4583333333333), np.float64(1476.5624999999998)), (np.float64(561.4583333333333), np.float64(1939.5833333333333)), (np.float64(2617.708333333333), np.float64(1939.5833333333333)), (np.float64(2617.708333333333), np.float64(1476.5624999999998))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 79, 'image_path': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-79-31.jpg', 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'category': 'Image', 'element_id': 'f5fc469a4d99eb6999604ac5779bd725', 'source_image': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-79-31.jpg'}, page_content='[IMAGE DESCRIPTION]\nSource File: /Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-79-31.jpg\nThe image is a diagram illustrating the structure of a Satellite Virtual Network (SVN). It is divided into several sections, each representing different components and protocols involved in the network. Here is a detailed description:\n\n1. **Title and Direction**:\n   - At the top of the image, the title "Satellite Virtual Network (SVN)" is written in bold text.\n   - A blue arrow spans the entire width of the image, indicating the direction of the network flow from left to right.\n\n2. **Left Section**:\n   - The first section on the left is labeled "IPVPN" (Internet Protocol Virtual Private Network).\n   - Below this label, there is a depiction of a private network, represented by a laptop connected to a router.\n   - The router is connected to a backbone network, which is represented by another router.\n\n3. **Middle Section**:\n   - The second section is labeled "MPLS IPVPN" (Multiprotocol Label Switching IPVPN).\n   - This section shows an access router connected to a SAS LAN (Satellite Access System Local Area Network).\n   - The SAS LAN is connected to a PP (Point-to-Point) device, which is part of the OTA (Onboard Terminal Access) Satellite Network.\n\n4. **Right Section**:\n   - The third section is labeled "VRFLite IPVPN" (Virtual Routing and Forwarding Lite IPVPN).\n   - This section shows a terminal connected to a Terminal LAN (Local Area Network).\n   - The Terminal LAN is connected to a subnet, which is represented by a laptop connected to a router.\n\n5. **Further Right Section**:\n   - The fourth section is labeled "VRFLite IPVPN" again.\n   - This section shows a VLAN/Ethernet connection, which is part of the satellite network.\n   - The VLAN/Ethernet connection is connected to the OTA Satellite Network.\n\n6. **Far Right Section**:\n   - The final section is labeled "IP" (Internet Protocol).\n   - This section shows a subnet connected to a router, which is part of the private network.\n\nThe diagram uses ellipses to represent different network segments and routers to connect these segments. The overall flow of the network is indicated by the blue arrow, which moves from the IPVPN section on the left to the IP section on the right.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(576.0416666666666), np.float64(758.3333333333333)), (np.float64(576.0416666666666), np.float64(1866.6666666666665)), (np.float64(2614.0625), np.float64(1866.6666666666665)), (np.float64(2614.0625), np.float64(758.3333333333333))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 80, 'image_path': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-80-32.jpg', 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'category': 'Image', 'element_id': 'eff40df9deab917431e8abd5fc977d6a', 'source_image': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-80-32.jpg'}, page_content='[IMAGE DESCRIPTION]\nSource File: /Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-80-32.jpg\nThe image is a detailed diagram illustrating the architecture of a Satellite Virtual Network (SVN). It is divided into two main sections: an "Example" section at the top and a "Pulse Representation" section at the bottom.\n\n### Example Section:\n- **Title:** Satellite Virtual Network (SVN)\n- **Arrows:** Two blue arrows at the top indicate the direction of the network flow.\n- **Nodes and Connections:**\n  - **Private Network:** Connected to a backbone network.\n  - **Backbone Network:** Connected to an Access Router.\n  - **Access Router:** Connected to a SAS LAN.\n  - **SAS LAN:** Connected to a PP (Point-to-Point) device.\n  - **PP Device:** Connected to an OTA (Onboard Terminal) Satellite Network.\n  - **OTA Satellite Network:** Connected to a Terminal.\n  - **Terminal:** Connected to a Terminal LAN.\n  - **Terminal LAN:** Connected to a Subnet.\n  - **Subnet:** Connected to a private network.\n\n### Pulse Representation Section:\n- **Title:** Pulse Representation\n- **Arrows:** Two blue arrows at the bottom indicate the direction of the network flow.\n- **Nodes and Connections:**\n  - **Private Network:** Connected to an IPVPN.\n  - **IPVPN:** Connected to a backbone network.\n  - **Backbone Network:** Connected to an Access Router.\n  - **Access Router:** Connected to a SAS LAN.\n  - **SAS LAN:** Connected to a PP (Point-to-Point) device.\n  - **PP Device:** Connected to an OTA (Onboard Terminal) Satellite Network.\n  - **OTA Satellite Network:** Connected to a Terminal.\n  - **Terminal:** Connected to a Terminal LAN.\n  - **Terminal LAN:** Connected to a Subnet.\n  - **Subnet:** Connected to an IP.\n\n### Key Features:\n- **SVN Types:**\n  - **Site SVN:** Represents the network at a specific site.\n  - **Terminal SVN:** Represents the network at a terminal location.\n  - **Global SVN:** Represents the overall network architecture.\n\n- **Technologies Used:**\n  - IPVPN\n  - MPLS IPVPN\n  - VRFLite IPVPN\n  - VLAN / Ethernet\n  - VLAN / Satellite\n\n- **Components:**\n  - Private Network\n  - Backbone Network\n  - Access Router\n  - SAS LAN\n  - PP Device\n  - OTA Satellite Network\n  - Terminal\n  - Terminal LAN\n  - Subnet\n\nThe diagram uses arrows and ellipses to represent the flow and connections within the network, with labels indicating the type of network technology and the specific components involved.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(568.75), np.float64(1374.4791666666665)), (np.float64(568.75), np.float64(2446.3541666666665)), (np.float64(2625.0), np.float64(2446.3541666666665)), (np.float64(2625.0), np.float64(1374.4791666666665))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 82, 'image_path': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-82-33.jpg', 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'category': 'Image', 'element_id': '41f493a8cd28e80ba622f0ff5ddbf241', 'source_image': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-82-33.jpg'}, page_content='[IMAGE DESCRIPTION]\nSource File: /Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-82-33.jpg\nThe image is a detailed network diagram illustrating a network architecture, likely used in a telecommunications or data center context. Here is a breakdown of the elements and their relationships:\n\n### Main Components:\n1. **Backbone Network**: This is the central part of the network, represented by a large blue circle. It connects various components and serves as the main pathway for data traffic.\n2. **Access Router**: These are connected to the backbone network and are responsible for routing traffic to and from the backbone.\n3. **Protocol Processor (PP)**: These devices are connected to the backbone network and are responsible for processing and forwarding data.\n4. **Satellite Terminal (ST)**: These are connected to the backbone network and are used to connect remote locations.\n5. **Router (Rtr)**: These devices are connected to the backbone network and are used to route traffic between different parts of the network.\n6. **Customer Network**: This is the network that connects to the backbone network and is used to provide services to customers.\n7. **Customer Equipment**: This is the end-user equipment that connects to the customer network.\n\n### Network Layers and Protocols:\n1. **VRF-lite**: This is a virtual routing and forwarding technology used to isolate traffic for different virtual networks.\n2. **BGP Session per PP and SVN**: This indicates that Border Gateway Protocol sessions are established between the Protocol Processors (PP) and the Satellite Virtual Networks (SVN).\n3. **BGP Session per SVN**: This indicates that Border Gateway Protocol sessions are established between the Satellite Virtual Networks (SVN).\n4. **VLAN per SVN**: This indicates that Virtual Local Area Networks are configured for each Satellite Virtual Network (SVN).\n\n### Remote Locations:\n- The diagram shows multiple remote locations connected to the backbone network via Satellite Terminals (ST) and Routers (Rtr). Each remote location has its own VLAN per SVN, indicating that each location has its own isolated network segment.\n\n### Key:\n- **BGP**: Border Gateway Protocol\n- **PP**: Protocol Processor\n- **Rtr**: Router\n- **ST**: Satellite Terminal\n- **SVN**: Satellite Virtual Network (VPN)\n- **VRF**: Virtual Routing and Forwarding\n\n### Diagram Structure:\n- The backbone network is the central hub, with access routers and protocol processors connected to it.\n- The remote locations are connected to the backbone network via satellite terminals and routers.\n- The VLAN per SVN and BGP session per SVN are used to ensure that traffic is properly routed and isolated within the network.\n\nThis diagram is a comprehensive representation of a network architecture designed to manage and route traffic efficiently, with a focus on isolating traffic for different virtual networks and ensuring secure and reliable communication.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(597.9166666666666), np.float64(2701.5625)), (np.float64(597.9166666666666), np.float64(2876.5624999999995)), (np.float64(772.9166666666666), np.float64(2876.5624999999995)), (np.float64(772.9166666666666), np.float64(2701.5625))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 82, 'image_path': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-82-34.jpg', 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'category': 'Image', 'element_id': 'af6296d23256c5214b109d712bd62c6a', 'source_image': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-82-34.jpg'}, page_content='[IMAGE DESCRIPTION]\nSource File: /Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-82-34.jpg\nThe image appears to be a pixelated representation of a blue rectangular shape with a white interior. The edges of the rectangle are jagged, indicating that the image is low-resolution or has been pixelated. There is no text, numbers, charts, or diagrams visible within the rectangle. The overall appearance suggests that this might be a simplified or abstract representation of a shape, possibly a gift box or a similar object, given the shape and the way it is presented. The blue color could represent a ribbon or a bow, which is often associated with gift wrapping.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(681.7708333333333), np.float64(2161.9791666666665)), (np.float64(681.7708333333333), np.float64(2982.2916666666665)), (np.float64(2511.9791666666665), np.float64(2982.2916666666665)), (np.float64(2511.9791666666665), np.float64(2161.9791666666665))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 86, 'image_path': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-86-35.jpg', 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'category': 'Image', 'element_id': '6613deb3a53846aae4da0e82df2938fa', 'source_image': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-86-35.jpg'}, page_content='[IMAGE DESCRIPTION]\nSource File: /Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-86-35.jpg\nThe image is a detailed diagram illustrating the architecture and flow of multicast traffic in a network system, particularly focusing on the use of Internet Group Management Protocol (IGMP) versions 2 and 3. Here is a breakdown of the components and their roles:\n\n### Top Section:\n1. **Multicast Source**: This is the origin of the multicast traffic. It can be a backbone network, a customer private network, or the Internet.\n2. **Backbone, Customer Private Network, and/or Internet**: This represents the network infrastructure that the multicast traffic travels through.\n3. **Access Router**: This is a device that connects the backbone network to the SAS LAN (Satellite Access System Local Area Network).\n4. **SAS LAN**: This is the local area network within the satellite access system.\n5. **Switch**: This is a networking device used to connect various components within the SAS LAN.\n6. **NMS (Network Management System)**: This is a system used for monitoring and managing the network.\n7. **PP (Proxy Proxy)**: This is a device that acts as a proxy for the IGMP traffic.\n8. **IGMP Proxy**: This is a specific type of proxy used to manage IGMP traffic.\n9. **Tx/Rx Line Cards**: These are line cards used for transmitting and receiving data.\n10. **OTA (Over-the-Air)**: This indicates the transmission of data over the air, typically via satellite.\n11. **Satellite Terminal**: This is the device that receives the multicast traffic from the satellite.\n12. **IGMP Proxy**: This is another IGMP proxy used within the satellite terminal.\n13. **Terminal LAN**: This is the local area network at the terminal end.\n14. **Multicast Listener**: This is a device or application that listens for multicast traffic.\n\n### Bottom Section:\n1. **Dynamic Multicast**: This is the process where the multicast traffic is dynamically managed.\n2. **Static Multicast**: This is the process where the multicast traffic is statically configured.\n3. **Persistent Multicast**: This is the process where the multicast traffic is persistent.\n4. **IGMPv3**: This is the version of IGMP used for the multicast traffic.\n5. **IGMPv2 or IGMPv3**: This indicates the version of IGMP used for the multicast traffic at the host level.\n6. **IGMP Router**: This is a device that manages IGMP traffic.\n7. **IGMP Host**: This is a device or application that is a host for the multicast traffic.\n\n### Flow of Multicast Traffic:\n- The multicast traffic starts from the Multicast Source.\n- It travels through the backbone network, customer private network, or the Internet.\n- It then enters the Access Router and is forwarded to the SAS LAN.\n- Within the SAS LAN, the traffic is managed by the PP and IGMP Proxy.\n- The traffic is then transmitted over the air via the OTA to the Satellite Terminal.\n- At the Satellite Terminal, the traffic is managed by another IGMP Proxy.\n- Finally, the traffic reaches the Terminal LAN and is received by the Multicast Listener.\n\nThis diagram effectively illustrates the flow and management of multicast traffic in a satellite-based network system, highlighting the use of IGMP versions 2 and 3 for different types of multicast traffic (dynamic, static, and persistent).'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(634.375), np.float64(430.2083333333333)), (np.float64(634.375), np.float64(1214.0625)), (np.float64(2555.7291666666665), np.float64(1214.0625)), (np.float64(2555.7291666666665), np.float64(430.2083333333333))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 89, 'image_path': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-89-36.jpg', 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'category': 'Image', 'element_id': '845441ec7cdeae452e48f440a928799a', 'source_image': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-89-36.jpg'}, page_content='[IMAGE DESCRIPTION]\nSource File: /Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-89-36.jpg\nThe image illustrates the architecture of an iDirect Velocity SAS (Satellite Access System) network. Here is a detailed description of the components and their arrangement:\n\n1. **SP Core Network**: This is the starting point of the network, represented by a cloud icon with multiple access routers connected to it. The access routers are depicted as blue circular icons with arrows pointing outward, indicating their role in connecting to the core network.\n\n2. **Access Routers**: These are the devices that connect the SP Core Network to the core of the iDirect Velocity SAS architecture. They are shown as blue circular icons with arrows pointing outward.\n\n3. **Nexus 5k Core Switch**: This is the central switch in the core of the network. It is depicted as a blue square icon with a starburst symbol, indicating its role as a core switch. There are three Nexus 5k Core Switches shown in the diagram.\n\n4. **Nexus 2k Fabric Extender**: These are the devices that extend the fabric of the network. They are depicted as blue square icons with a starburst symbol. There are four Nexus 2k Fabric Extenders shown in the diagram, each connected to the Nexus 5k Core Switches.\n\n5. **Line Card Chassis**: These are the devices that provide the physical connection to the satellite. They are depicted as white rectangular icons with a grid of ports. There are two Line Card Chassis shown in the diagram, each connected to the Nexus 2k Fabric Extenders.\n\n6. **NMS (Network Management System)**: This is the system that manages the network. It is depicted as a white rectangular icon with a grid of ports. It is connected to the Line Card Chassis.\n\n7. **Protocol Processors**: These are the devices that handle the protocol processing. They are depicted as white rectangular icons with a grid of ports. There are two Protocol Processors shown in the diagram, each connected to the Line Card Chassis.\n\n8. **SAS (Satellite Access System)**: This is the satellite access system. It is depicted as a white rectangular icon with a grid of ports. It is connected to the Line Card Chassis.\n\nThe diagram is structured in a way that shows the flow of data from the SP Core Network through the Access Routers, Nexus 5k Core Switches, Nexus 2k Fabric Extenders, Line Card Chassis, Protocol Processors, and finally to the SAS. The connections are represented by blue lines, indicating the data flow. The diagram is enclosed in a green border with the text "iDirect Velocity SAS Architecture" at the top.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(765.6249999999999), np.float64(758.3333333333333)), (np.float64(765.6249999999999), np.float64(1644.2708333333333)), (np.float64(2428.125), np.float64(1644.2708333333333)), (np.float64(2428.125), np.float64(758.3333333333333))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 90, 'image_path': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/fi