n\n1. **Circles and Labels**:\n   - There are three circles in the diagram, each labeled with a "Beam" number.\n   - The largest circle is labeled "Beam 1" and is colored in a light yellow shade.\n   - The second circle, partially overlapping with "Beam 1," is labeled "Beam 2" and is colored in a light pink shade.\n   - The third circle, overlapping with both "Beam 1" and "Beam 2," is labeled "Beam 3" and is colored in a light blue shade.\n\n2. **Overlapping Areas**:\n   - The area where "Beam 1" and "Beam 2" overlap is labeled "Beam 2."\n   - The area where "Beam 2" and "Beam 3" overlap is labeled "Beam 3."\n   - The area where "Beam 1," "Beam 2," and "Beam 3" all overlap is not labeled with a specific "Beam" number but represents the intersection of all three beams.\n\n3. **Background and Style**:\n   - The background of the image is white.\n   - The circles are drawn with a thin black outline.\n   - The text within the circles is in a bold, black font.\n\nThis Venn diagram visually represents the relationships and intersections between three different beams, with the largest circle representing the most general set and the smallest circle representing the most specific set.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(853.1249999999999), np.float64(1031.7708333333333)), (np.float64(853.1249999999999), np.float64(2056.25)), (np.float64(2340.625), np.float64(2056.25)), (np.float64(2340.625), np.float64(1031.7708333333333))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 118, 'image_path': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-118-63.jpg', 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'category': 'Image', 'element_id': '4fc0c3ebc65f450d58670be4873b7bdb', 'source_image': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-118-63.jpg'}, page_content='[IMAGE DESCRIPTION]\nSource File: /Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-118-63.jpg\nThe image is a diagram illustrating the concept of satellite communication, specifically focusing on the uplink and downlink processes. Here is a detailed description:\n\n1. **Satellite and Antenna**:\n   - At the top of the diagram, there is a satellite depicted with multiple antennas. These antennas are arranged in a way that suggests they are capable of sending and receiving signals in different directions.\n\n2. **Uplink Per Beam**:\n   - The text "uplink per beam" is written near the satellite, indicating that the satellite is capable of receiving signals from multiple ground stations or antennas. The arrows pointing towards the satellite from the ground station represent the uplink signals.\n\n3. **Ground Station**:\n   - Below the satellite, there is a ground station represented by a large satellite dish. The dish is connected to three vertical lines, which are labeled as "one hub line card per uplink." This suggests that the ground station is equipped with multiple line cards to handle the uplink signals from different beams.\n\n4. **Downlink Per Beam**:\n   - The text "downlink per beam" is written near the satellite, indicating that the satellite is capable of sending signals to different areas on the ground. The arrows pointing from the satellite to the ground represent the downlink signals.\n\n5. **Ground Coverage Areas**:\n   - Below the satellite, there are three overlapping circles representing different ground coverage areas. These circles are labeled with different colors (light pink, light orange, and light yellow) and are connected to the satellite by arrows. This indicates that the satellite can send downlink signals to different regions on the ground simultaneously.\n\n6. **Beam Formation**:\n   - The diagram shows that the satellite uses multiple beams to cover different areas on the ground. The beams are formed by the satellite\'s antennas, and each beam is capable of sending and receiving signals independently.\n\nIn summary, the diagram illustrates a satellite communication system where the satellite uses multiple beams to cover different areas on the ground. The ground station is equipped with multiple line cards to handle the uplink signals from different beams, and the satellite sends downlink signals to the ground in a way that ensures coverage of multiple regions simultaneously.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(743.7499999999999), np.float64(1031.7708333333333)), (np.float64(743.7499999999999), np.float64(2326.0416666666665)), (np.float64(2450.0), np.float64(2326.0416666666665)), (np.float64(2450.0), np.float64(1031.7708333333333))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 119, 'image_path': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-119-64.jpg', 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'category': 'Image', 'element_id': '60261eed0a66a30fdb2debf65f4e1fe8', 'source_image': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-119-64.jpg'}, page_content='[IMAGE DESCRIPTION]\nSource File: /Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-119-64.jpg\nThe image illustrates a satellite communication system, specifically focusing on the uplink and downlink processes. Here\'s a detailed description:\n\n1. **Satellite and Uplink**:\n   - At the top of the image, there is a depiction of a satellite in space. The satellite is shown with multiple beams radiating from it, indicating its capability to transmit signals to various ground stations.\n   - Below the satellite, there is a ground station represented by a large dish antenna. This antenna is connected to a hub line card, which is indicated by the text "one hub line card" near the bottom left corner of the image.\n   - The text "one uplink" is written along the line connecting the ground station to the satellite, indicating that this is the direction of the uplink signal from the ground station to the satellite.\n\n2. **Downlink**:\n   - The satellite sends signals in multiple directions, as indicated by the multiple beams radiating from it. These beams are labeled as "downlink per beam," suggesting that the satellite transmits data to different ground stations or regions using these beams.\n   - The beams are shown intersecting with different colored circles, which represent the coverage areas of the satellite. These circles are labeled with different colors (light blue, light yellow, light green, and light pink), indicating different regions or sectors that the satellite can cover.\n\n3. **Coverage Areas**:\n   - The colored circles represent the coverage areas of the satellite. Each color corresponds to a different sector or region that the satellite can communicate with. The text "downlink per beam" is written near each beam, emphasizing that the satellite sends downlink signals to these regions.\n\nIn summary, the image depicts a satellite communication system where a ground station sends an uplink signal to the satellite, which then transmits downlink signals to different regions or sectors, as indicated by the multiple beams and colored coverage areas.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(776.5624999999999), np.float64(1250.5208333333333)), (np.float64(776.5624999999999), np.float64(2450.0)), (np.float64(2413.5416666666665), np.float64(2450.0)), (np.float64(2413.5416666666665), np.float64(1250.5208333333333))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 120, 'image_path': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-120-65.jpg', 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'category': 'Image', 'element_id': 'df0701576212c313f50e41c7ac824d9a', 'source_image': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-120-65.jpg'}, page_content='[IMAGE DESCRIPTION]\nSource File: /Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-120-65.jpg\nThe image is a diagram illustrating the concept of uplinks and downlinks in a network system, specifically labeled as "iNet." Here is a detailed description:\n\n1. **Satellite Dish and Hub Line Cards:**\n   - On the left side of the image, there is a satellite dish pointing towards a cluster of rectangular shapes labeled as "one TX and one or more Rx hub line cards per iNet."\n   - The satellite dish is connected to the hub line cards, which are depicted as a series of rectangular blocks.\n\n2. **Uplinks and Downlinks:**\n   - Arrows are drawn from the satellite dish to the hub line cards, indicating the direction of data transmission. These arrows are labeled as "Uplinks and downlinks per iNet."\n   - The arrows show the flow of data from the satellite dish to the hub line cards, which suggests that the hub line cards are responsible for receiving and transmitting data.\n\n3. **Downlinks and Uplinks:**\n   - On the right side of the image, there are three overlapping circles, each representing a different network segment or user group.\n   - Arrows are drawn from the hub line cards to the overlapping circles, indicating the direction of data transmission to these segments. These arrows are labeled as "Downlinks & uplinks per iNet."\n   - The overlapping circles suggest that the hub line cards are serving multiple users or segments, and the arrows indicate the direction of data flow to these segments.\n\n4. **Overall Structure:**\n   - The diagram illustrates a network architecture where the satellite dish acts as a transmitter (TX) and the hub line cards act as receivers (Rx) for the iNet system.\n   - The hub line cards then distribute the received data to multiple users or segments, as indicated by the overlapping circles and the arrows pointing to them.\n\nThis diagram is likely used to explain the functionality of a network system where data is transmitted from a satellite to a hub, which then distributes the data to various users or segments.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(586.9791666666666), np.float64(1122.9166666666665)), (np.float64(586.9791666666666), np.float64(1782.8124999999998)), (np.float64(2603.125), np.float64(1782.8124999999998)), (np.float64(2603.125), np.float64(1122.9166666666665))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 123, 'image_path': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-123-66.jpg', 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'category': 'Image', 'element_id': '13c1c9e0a109743b48ec6d2b034c8cc3', 'source_image': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-123-66.jpg'}, page_content='[IMAGE DESCRIPTION]\nSource File: /Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-123-66.jpg\nThe image is a diagram illustrating the structure of a Baseband Frame in a communication system, specifically labeled as "Short (ACM)." Here is a detailed description of the components and their labels:\n\n1. **Baseband Frame**: This is the overarching structure of the frame, which contains all the other components.\n\n2. **16,200 coded bits**: This indicates the total number of bits in the Baseband Frame.\n\n3. **Baseband (BB) Frame**: This is a substructure within the Baseband Frame, which is further divided into several components.\n\n4. **BB Hdr (10)**: This represents the Baseband Header, which is 10 bits long.\n\n5. **Data**: This is the main data payload of the frame, which is the largest component and is colored red.\n\n6. **CRC-32**: This stands for Cyclic Redundancy Check, a 32-bit checksum used for error detection.\n\n7. **Padding (opt)**: This is an optional padding field, which can be added to the frame.\n\n8. **FEC**: This stands for Forward Error Correction, which is used to correct errors in the transmission.\n\n9. **Multiple LEGS**: This indicates that the Data section can be divided into multiple segments, labeled as LEGS 1, LEGS 2, ..., LEGS n.\n\n10. **LEGS 1, LEGS 2, ..., LEGS n**: These are the individual segments of the Data section, which can vary in length depending on the MODCOD (Modulation and Coding Scheme) used.\n\n11. **Variable length depending on MODCOD BCH + LDPC FEC**: This text explains that the length of the FEC field can vary based on the specific MODCOD scheme used, which includes BCH (Bose-Chaudhuri-Hocquenghem) and LDPC (Low-Density Parity-Check) FEC (Forward Error Correction) codes.\n\nThe diagram uses different colors to highlight different components: green for headers and padding, red for the data, and blue for the FEC field. The structure is designed to ensure reliable transmission of data by including error detection and correction mechanisms.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(590.625), np.float64(1246.875)), (np.float64(590.625), np.float64(2107.2916666666665)), (np.float64(2599.4791666666665), np.float64(2107.2916666666665)), (np.float64(2599.4791666666665), np.float64(1246.875))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 124, 'image_path': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-124-67.jpg', 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'category': 'Image', 'element_id': 'cfc0f5371f5912f444b90df2a7d1ea50', 'source_image': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-124-67.jpg'}, page_content='[IMAGE DESCRIPTION]\nSource File: /Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-124-67.jpg\nThe image is a comparison of two modes in DVB-S2: the CCM (Constant Coding and Modulation) Mode and the ACM (Adaptive Coding and Modulation) Mode. The image is divided into two sections, each illustrating the different approaches to coding and modulation.\n\n### CCM Mode:\n- **Text at the top:** "DVB-S2: CCM Mode"\n- **Description:** "All BBFrames: single MODCOD (one of QPSK 1/4, ..., 16PSK 9/10)"\n- **Diagram:**\n  - A horizontal timeline labeled "time" is shown.\n  - The timeline is divided into segments, each representing a BBFrame (Baseband Frame).\n  - Each segment is labeled with "8PSK" and "8/9," indicating that the same modulation and coding scheme (8PSK with a code rate of 8/9) is used consistently across all BBFrames.\n\n### ACM Mode:\n- **Text at the top:** "DVB-S2: ACM Mode"\n- **Description:** "Each BBFrame: potentially different MODCOD (any of QPSK 1/4, ..., 16PSK 9/10)"\n- **Diagram:**\n  - A horizontal timeline labeled "time" is shown.\n  - The timeline is divided into segments, each representing a BBFrame.\n  - Each segment is labeled with different modulation and coding schemes:\n    - 16P with a code rate of 5/6\n    - 16P with a code rate of 4/5\n    - 8PSK with a code rate of 2/3\n    - 16P with a code rate of 4/5\n    - 8PSK with a code rate of 2/3\n    - 8PSK with a code rate of 8/9\n    - 8PSK with a code rate of 8/9\n    - 16P with a code rate of 8/9\n    - 8PSK with a code rate of 3/4\n\n### Summary:\n- The CCM Mode uses a single modulation and coding scheme (8PSK with a code rate of 8/9) consistently across all BBFrames.\n- The ACM Mode allows for different modulation and coding schemes in each BBFrame, providing flexibility and potentially better performance in varying channel conditions.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(568.75), np.float64(430.2083333333333)), (np.float64(568.75), np.float64(718.2291666666666)), (np.float64(2625.0), np.float64(718.2291666666666)), (np.float64(2625.0), np.float64(430.2083333333333))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 125, 'image_path': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-125-68.jpg', 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'category': 'Image', 'element_id': 'eb98e2de5277e35978a61bbf764f5b6d', 'source_image': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-125-68.jpg'}, page_content='[IMAGE DESCRIPTION]\nSource File: /Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-125-68.jpg\nThe image is a diagram illustrating the structure of a signal frame, which is divided into three main sections: PLHEADER, Pilot symbols, and Data symbols. Here is a detailed description:\n\n1. **PLHEADER Section:**\n   - This section is labeled as "PLHEADER: signals MODCOD and frame length (always π/2 BPSK)."\n   - It consists of a series of red and green bars.\n   - The red bars are labeled as "MODCOD and frame length," indicating that this part of the signal frame contains information about the modulation coding scheme (MODCOD) and the frame length.\n   - The text specifies that this section always uses π/2 BPSK (Binary Phase-Shift Keying) modulation.\n\n2. **Pilot Symbols Section:**\n   - This section is labeled as "Pilot symbols: unmodulated carrier."\n   - It consists of a series of green bars.\n   - The green bars represent pilot symbols, which are used for synchronization and channel estimation purposes.\n   - The text indicates that these symbols are unmodulated carriers, meaning they do not carry any data but are used to help the receiver track the signal and estimate the channel conditions.\n\n3. **Data Symbols Section:**\n   - This section is labeled as "Data symbols: QPSK, 8PSK, or 16APSK."\n   - It consists of a series of green bars.\n   - The green bars represent data symbols, which carry the actual data being transmitted.\n   - The text specifies that these data symbols can be modulated using QPSK (Quadrature Phase-Shift Keying), 8PSK (8-Phase-Shift Keying), or 16APSK (16-Asymmetrical Phase-Shift Keying).\n\nThe diagram uses a combination of red and green bars to visually represent the different types of symbols within each section of the signal frame. The red bars in the PLHEADER section and the green bars in the Pilot symbols and Data symbols sections are used to distinguish between the different types of information being transmitted.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folde