ted in yellow, while the "Payload" blocks are in gray.\n   - The "Payload Blk 1" is followed by "Pilot Blk 2," then "Payload Blk 2," and so on, up to "Payload Blk Np," which is followed by "Pilot Blk Np," and finally "Payload Blk n+1."\n\n3. **Numbers and Variables**:\n   - The blocks are numbered with "Blk 1," "Blk 2," "Blk 3," and so on, up to "Blk Np."\n   - The "Payload Blk Np" and "Payload Blk n+1" suggest a pattern where the number of payload blocks can vary, with "Np" and "n+1" indicating a variable number of payload blocks.\n\n4. **Overall Pattern**:\n   - The diagram shows a repeating pattern of "Pilot" and "Payload" blocks, with the "Payload" blocks increasing in number up to "Payload Blk Np" and then continuing with "Payload Blk n+1."\n\nThis diagram is likely used to illustrate a specific protocol or standard in communication systems, such as OFDM (Orthogonal Frequency Division Multiplexing) or a similar modulation technique, where pilots are used for synchronization and payload blocks carry the actual data.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(882.2916666666666), np.float64(820.3124999999999)), (np.float64(882.2916666666666), np.float64(1596.8749999999998)), (np.float64(2307.8125), np.float64(1596.8749999999998)), (np.float64(2307.8125), np.float64(820.3124999999999))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 136, 'image_path': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-136-77.jpg', 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'category': 'Image', 'element_id': '3bf8ae146716f2af1bdf04d9313530e8', 'source_image': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-136-77.jpg'}, page_content='[IMAGE DESCRIPTION]\nSource File: /Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-136-77.jpg\nThe image is a diagram illustrating the concept of "Inroute Groups" in a communication system, specifically focusing on the use of different modulation and coding schemes (MODCODs) and symbol rates. The diagram is divided into two sections, each representing a different scenario.\n\n### Top Section:\n- **Title**: "Inroute Groups consisting of homogeneous carriers"\n- **Content**:\n  - There are six identical blue boxes, each labeled "QPSK 3/4".\n  - Each box represents a carrier with the same symbol rate, MODCOD, SS factors, and payload size.\n  - The text on the right side of the top section lists the following characteristics:\n    - Same symbol rates\n    - Same MODCODs\n    - Same SS factors\n    - Same payload size\n\n### Bottom Section:\n- **Title**: "Inroute Groups can consist of heterogeneous carriers"\n- **Content**:\n  - There are seven boxes of different colors and labels, representing different carriers with varying characteristics.\n    - Two green boxes labeled "8PSK 2/3".\n    - One blue box labeled "QPSK 3/4".\n    - Two more blue boxes labeled "QP SK 3/4" and "QP SK 1/2".\n    - One yellow box labeled "BPSK 1/2 SF=4".\n  - Each box represents a carrier with different symbol rates, MODCODs, SS factors, and payload sizes.\n  - The text on the right side of the bottom section lists the following characteristics:\n    - Different symbol rates\n    - Different MODCODs\n    - Different SS factors\n    - Same payload size\n\n### Summary:\nThe diagram contrasts two scenarios of inroute groups:\n1. **Homogeneous Carriers**: All carriers have the same symbol rate, MODCOD, SS factors, and payload size.\n2. **Heterogeneous Carriers**: Carriers have different symbol rates, MODCODs, SS factors, but the same payload size.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(1046.3541666666665), np.float64(1410.9374999999998)), (np.float64(1046.3541666666665), np.float64(2132.8125)), (np.float64(2147.395833333333), np.float64(2132.8125)), (np.float64(2147.395833333333), np.float64(1410.9374999999998))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 138, 'image_path': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-138-78.jpg', 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'category': 'Image', 'element_id': '5a845555fcaf5ce4ff52aa8c9649068e', 'source_image': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-138-78.jpg'}, page_content='[IMAGE DESCRIPTION]\nSource File: /Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-138-78.jpg\nThe image is a flowchart that illustrates a process for managing satellite terminals and their resource allocation. Here is a detailed description of the components and their relationships:\n\n1. **Satellite Terminals**:\n   - At the top left, there is a circular diagram labeled "Satellite Terminals." Inside this circle, there are icons representing satellite terminals, which are connected to a central hub.\n   - The satellite terminals are connected to a box labeled "Power, signal quality," indicating the factors that influence the performance of the terminals.\n\n2. **Capacity Requests**:\n   - Arrows point from the satellite terminals to a box labeled "Capacity Requests," indicating that the terminals send requests for capacity.\n\n3. **Short-term adaptivity**:\n   - A box labeled "Short-term adaptivity" follows the "Capacity Requests" box. This box contains the text: "Determine which remotes can use which carriers in current group."\n   - This step involves deciding which remote terminals can use which carriers within the current group, based on the capacity requests and the power and signal quality of the terminals.\n\n4. **Real-time resource management**:\n   - Another box labeled "Real-time resource management" follows the "Short-term adaptivity" box. This box contains the text: "Assign slots in current group to remotes, respecting constraints on the carriers they can use."\n   - This step involves assigning slots to the remote terminals within the current group, ensuring that the constraints on the carriers they can use are respected.\n\n5. **Medium-term adaptivity**:\n   - A box labeled "Medium-term adaptivity" follows the "Real-time resource management" box. This box contains the text: "Select best among defined sets of carriers."\n   - This step involves selecting the best set of carriers from predefined sets, based on the real-time resource management and the current group configuration.\n\n6. **Carrier group configuration**:\n   - Below the "Medium-term adaptivity" box, there are three arrows pointing to a section labeled "Carrier group configuration 1," "Carrier group configuration 2," and "Carrier group configuration n." These sections represent different configurations of carrier groups.\n   - The arrows indicate that the medium-term adaptivity step leads to the selection of the best carrier group configuration.\n\n7. **Long-term adaptivity**:\n   - A box labeled "Long-term adaptivity" is on the far right. This box contains the text: "Design most suitable set of carrier configurations."\n   - This step involves designing the most suitable set of carrier configurations based on the statistics and the results from the medium-term adaptivity step.\n\n8. **Statistics**:\n   - An arrow points from the "Long-term adaptivity" box to a box labeled "Statistics," indicating that the long-term adaptivity process uses statistical data to inform the design of the most suitable carrier configurations.\n\nThe flowchart illustrates a hierarchical decision-making process for managing satellite terminals, from short-term adaptivity to long-term adaptivity, ensuring that the resource allocation is optimized and constraints are respected.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(915.1041666666666), np.float64(2756.25)), (np.float64(915.1041666666666), np.float64(3288.5416666666665)), (np.float64(2275.0), np.float64(3288.5416666666665)), (np.float64(2275.0), np.float64(2756.25))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 138, 'image_path': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-138-79.jpg', 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'category': 'Image', 'element_id': 'ccd6d664191c634e87a229d13598bfe3', 'source_image': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-138-79.jpg'}, page_content='[IMAGE DESCRIPTION]\nSource File: /Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-138-79.jpg\nThe image is a diagram illustrating the concept of frequency hopping in satellite communication systems, specifically focusing on the allocation of timeslots across different carriers. Here\'s a detailed description:\n\n1. **Title and Axes**:\n   - The diagram has an x-axis labeled "Frequency (Hz)" and a y-axis labeled "Amplitude."\n   - The x-axis is divided into four segments, each representing a different carrier (Carrier-1, Carrier-2, Carrier-3, and Carrier-4).\n\n2. **Carriers and Frequencies**:\n   - **Carrier-1**: Located at the leftmost segment of the x-axis, with a frequency of 1000Ksps. The amplitude is shown as a large yellow area.\n   - **Carrier-2**: Located in the second segment, with a frequency of 500Ksps. The amplitude is also shown as a large yellow area.\n   - **Carrier-3**: Located in the third segment, with a frequency of 250Ksps. The amplitude is shown as a large yellow area.\n   - **Carrier-4**: Located in the fourth segment, with a frequency of 250Ksps. The amplitude is shown as a large yellow area.\n\n3. **Modulation and Coding**:\n   - Each carrier is associated with a specific modulation and coding scheme:\n     - Carrier-1: MODCOD 8PSK&7\n     - Carrier-2: MODCOD 8PSK&7\n     - Carrier-3: MODCOD 8PSK2&9\n     - Carrier-4: MODCOD QPSK3&4\n\n4. **C/No (Carrier-to-Noise Ratio)**:\n   - Below each carrier, there is a gray box indicating the required C/No (Carrier-to-Noise Ratio) in dBHz:\n     - Carrier-1: 74.1dBHz\n     - Carrier-2: 71.1dBHz\n     - Carrier-3: 64.0dBHz\n     - Carrier-4: 61.9dBHz\n\n5. **Satellite Routers and Frequency Hopping**:\n   - At the bottom of the image, there is a caption that reads: "Satellite Routers will receive timeslots in the most suitable carrier (frequency hopping)."\n\n6. **Visual Representation of Satellite Routers**:\n   - Below the caption, there are four icons representing satellite routers, each corresponding to the four carriers. The icons are black with a grid-like pattern.\n\nThis diagram is used to illustrate how satellite routers dynamically select the most suitable carrier for data transmission based on the available frequency bands and the required C/No.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(827.6041666666666), np.float64(2778.125)), (np.float64(827.6041666666666), np.float64(3434.3749999999995)), (np.float64(2366.145833333333), np.float64(3434.3749999999995)), (np.float64(2366.145833333333), np.float64(2778.125))