: '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-186-114.jpg'}, page_content='[IMAGE DESCRIPTION]\nSource File: /Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-186-114.jpg\nThe image is a bar chart titled "BW Required to Maintain a Fixed CIR across MODCODs." The chart illustrates the bandwidth (BW) required to maintain a fixed channel information rate (CIR) across various modulation and coding schemes (MODCODs). The x-axis lists different MODCODs, and the y-axis represents the bandwidth in units that are not explicitly labeled but can be inferred to be in bits per second (bps) or a similar unit.\n\nThe MODCODs listed on the x-axis include various combinations of 16APSK, 8PSK, and QPSK with different modulation orders and coding rates. For example, "16APSK 8/9" refers to a 16APSK modulation with a coding rate of 8/9, and "QPSK 1/4" refers to a quadrature phase shift keying (QPSK) modulation with a coding rate of 1/4.\n\nThe bars represent the bandwidth required for each MODCOD. The bandwidth values increase as the coding rate decreases, which is evident from the chart. For instance, "16APSK 8/9" has a lower bandwidth requirement compared to "QPSK 1/4," which has a higher bandwidth requirement.\n\nThe chart shows a clear trend where the bandwidth increases as the coding rate decreases, reflecting the trade-off between bandwidth and coding rate in communication systems. The highest bandwidth requirement is for "QPSK 1/4," while the lowest is for "16APSK 8/9."'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(619.7916666666666), np.float64(590.625)), (np.float64(619.7916666666666), np.float64(1717.1874999999998)), (np.float64(2570.3125), np.float64(1717.1874999999998)), (np.float64(2570.3125), np.float64(590.625))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 