ojects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-190-118.jpg\nThe image appears to be a pixelated representation of a blue rectangular shape with a white interior. The edges of the rectangle are jagged, indicating that the image is low-resolution or has been pixelated. There is no text, numbers, charts, or diagrams visible within the rectangle. The overall appearance suggests that this might be a simplified or abstract representation of a shape, possibly a gift box or a similar object, given the shape and the way it is presented. The blue color could represent a ribbon or a bow, which is often associated with gift wrapping.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(568.75), np.float64(430.2083333333333)), (np.float64(568.75), np.float64(1203.125)), (np.float64(2625.0), np.float64(1203.125)), (np.float64(2625.0), np.float64(430.2083333333333))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 191, 'image_path': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-191-119.jpg', 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'category': 'Image', 'element_id': '91806fb5d2eec0809b94d6c68f394609', 'source_image': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-191-119.jpg'}, page_content='[IMAGE DESCRIPTION]\nSource File: /Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-191-119.jpg\nThe image is a flowchart that outlines a process for determining the "Beam Average MODCOD" for a specific RF terminal type. Here is a detailed description of the flowchart:\n\n1. **Step 1: MDA Results for Beam C/N Distribution**\n   - The first box states: "MDA results for beam C/N distribution for availability 99% of the time (includes rain) for this RF terminal type."\n   - This step involves analyzing the MDA (Measurement Data Analysis) results for the carrier-to-noise ratio (C/N) distribution, ensuring that the availability is 99% of the time, which includes rain conditions.\n\n2. **Step 2: MDA Results for Beam MODCOD Distribution**\n   - The second box states: "MDA results for beam MODCOD distribution for availability 99% of the time for this RF terminal type."\n   - This step involves analyzing the MDA results for the MODCOD (Modulation and Coding Scheme) distribution, again ensuring that the availability is 99% of the time.\n\n3. **Step 3: Use the MODCOD Distribution & Carrier Capacity in MS/s to Get IP Rate**\n   - The third box states: "Use the MODCOD distribution & carrier capacity in MS/s to get IP rate – Call this \'Beam CIR\' for this RF terminal type."\n   - This step involves using the MODCOD distribution and the carrier capacity in megabytes per second (MB/s) to calculate the IP rate, which is then referred to as the "Beam CIR" (Circuit Impairment Rate) for the specific RF terminal type.\n\n4. **Step 4: Use "Beam CIR" in MB/s & Carrier Capacity in MS/s to Get "Beam Average MODCOD" for this RF Terminal Type**\n   - The fourth box states: "Use \'Beam CIR\' in MB/s & carrier capacity in MS/s to get \'Beam Average MODCOD\' for this RF terminal type."\n   - This step involves using the "Beam CIR" in MB/s and the carrier capacity in MS/s to calculate the "Beam Average MODCOD" for the specific RF terminal type.\n\n5. **Step 5: Use "Beam Average MODCOD" to Convert GSP CIR per Beam MB/s -> MS/s**\n   - The first oval at the bottom states: "Use \'Beam Average MODCOD\' to convert GSP CIR per beam MB/s -> MS/s."\n   - This step involves using the "Beam Average MODCOD" to convert the GSP CIR (Guaranteed Service Profile Circuit Impairment Rate) per beam from MB/s to MS/s.\n\n6. **Step 6: Use "Beam Average MODCOD" as Scaling Factor for this Beam in GSP Configuration**\n   - The second oval at the bottom states: "Use \'Beam Average MODCOD\' as scaling factor for this beam in GSP configuration."\n   - This step involves using the "Beam Average MODCOD" as a scaling factor for the specific beam in the GSP (Guaranteed Service Profile) configuration.\n\nThe flowchart is structured to guide through the process of determining the "Beam Average MODCOD" for an RF terminal type, ensuring that the calculations are based on the availability of 99% of the time, including rain conditions.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(568.75), np.float64(874.9999999999999)), (np.float64(568.75), np.float64(2264.0625)), (np.float64(2625.0), np.float64(2264.0625)), (np.float64(2625.0), np.float64(874.9999999999999))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 195, 'image_path': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-195-120.jpg', 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'category': 'Image', 'element_id': '891fd2fe4cada295625579cfd1227330', 'source_image': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-195-120.jpg'}, page_content='[IMAGE DESCRIPTION]\nSource File: /Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-195-120.jpg\nThe image is a detailed network diagram, likely representing a telecommunications or satellite communication system. Here\'s a breakdown of the elements present in the image:\n\n### Top Section:\n- **"Outbound Carrier Bandwidth"**: This is a label at the top of the diagram, indicating the primary resource being managed or distributed.\n- **Satellite Diagram**: On the right side, there is a simplified diagram of satellites, with lines extending from them to various points, suggesting the distribution of bandwidth or signals.\n\n### Main Section:\n- **Three Main Categories**:\n  1. **Unicast GSP 1** (Blue Box):\n     - Contains subcategories: GSP 1.1, GSP 1.2, GSP 1.3.\n     - These are connected to Terminals 17 and 18, with Subsystem PC (SSPC) labels (SSPC 1 and SSPC 4).\n  2. **Unicast GSP 2** (Red Box):\n     - Contains subcategories: GSP 2.1, GSP 2.2, GSP 2.3.\n     - These are connected to Terminal 19, with Subsystem PC (SSPC) labels (SSPC 2).\n  3. **Multicast M-GSP 3** (Yellow Box):\n     - Contains subcategories: GSP 3.1, GSP 3.2, GSP 3.3.\n     - These are connected to Terminals 18 and 19, with Multisubsystem PC (M-SSPC) labels (M-SSPC 5 and M-SSPC 6).\n\n### Right Section:\n- **Venn Diagrams**:\n  - There are multiple Venn diagrams on the right side, each representing the intersection of different GSPs (GSP 1, GSP 2, GSP 3) and their associated SSPCs (SSPC 1,'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(619.7916666666666), np.float64(430.2083333333333)), (np.float64(619.7916666666666), np.float64(1476.5624999999998)), (np.float64(2573.958333333333), np.float64(1476.5624999999998)), (np.float64(2573.958333333333), np.float64(430.2083333333333))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 196, 'image_path': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-196-121.jpg', 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'category': 'Image', 'element_id': '6753410d791b61d0e5e6c2b07044f7fe', 'source_image': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-196-121.jpg'}, page_content='[IMAGE DESCRIPTION]\nSource File: /Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-196-121.jpg\nThe image is a diagram illustrating a network architecture, specifically a Network Operations Center (NOC) with a focus on bandwidth management. Here\'s a detailed description:\n\n1. **Title and Overview**:\n   - The top of the image is labeled "NOC (Bandwidth Root)" indicating the central node of the network.\n\n2. **Nodes and Connections**:\n   - There are several nodes connected to the "NOC (Bandwidth Root)" at the top.\n   - The nodes are labeled as follows:\n     - M-GSP1\n     - M-GSP2\n     - M-GSP3\n     - GSP1\n     - GSP2\n   - These nodes are connected to the "NOC (Bandwidth Root)" via blue lines, indicating the flow of data or bandwidth.\n\n3. **Bandwidth Configuration**:\n   - Below the "NOC (Bandwidth Root)" and the connected nodes, there is a section labeled "SAS1 M-GSP1" and "SAS2 M-GSP1".\n   - These sections are further divided into five sub-nodes, each labeled as "iNet1 SAS1 M-GSP1", "iNet2 SAS1 M-GSP1", "iNet3 SAS1 M-GSP1", "iNet4 SAS1 M-GSP1", and "iNet5 SAS1 M-GSP1".\n   - Each of these sub-nodes is connected to the "SAS1 M-GSP1" and "SAS2 M-GSP1" nodes via dashed lines.\n   - The bandwidth configuration for these sub-nodes is specified as:\n     - CIR (Committed Information Rate) = 50 Mb/s\n     - MIR (Maximum Information Rate) = 70 Mb/s\n\n4. **Beam and Satellite Information**:\n   - Each sub-node is associated with a specific beam and satellite:\n     - iNet1 SAS1 M-GSP1 is associated with Beam 1 and Satellite 1.\n     - iNet2 SAS1 M-GSP1 is associated with Beam 2 and Satellite 1.\n     - iNet3 SAS1 M-GSP1 is associated with Beam 3 and Satellite 1.\n     - iNet4 SAS1 M-GSP1 is associated with Beam 4 and Satellite 1.\n     - iNet5 SAS1 M-GSP1 is associated with Beam 5 and Satellite 1.\n\n5. **Additional Information**:\n   - The bandwidth configuration (CIR and MIR) is noted as being "Configured in NMS" (Network Management System), indicating that these settings are managed through a network management system.\n\nThis diagram appears to be a detailed representation of a network architecture, focusing on bandwidth management and distribution across various nodes and sub-nodes, with specific configurations for each sub-node.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(947.9166666666666), np.float64(430.2083333333333)), (np.float64(947.9166666666666), np.float64(2413.5416666666665)), (np.float64(2245.833333333333), np.float64(2413.5416666666665)), (np.float64(2245.833333333333), np.float64(430.2083333333333))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 197, 'image_path': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-197-122.jpg', 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'category': 'Image', 'element_id': '3dd211d0f255e8f8b36c350201a8dd84', 'source_image': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-197-122.jpg'}, page_content='[IMAGE DESCRIPTION]\nSource File: /Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-197-122.jpg\nThe image is a diagram illustrating a network architecture related to Quality of Service (QoS) enforcement in a network system. Here is a detailed description of the elements in the image:\n\n1. **iNet QoS Enforcer**:\n   - At the top of the diagram, there is a circle labeled "iNet QoS Enforcer," which appears to be the central component responsible for managing Quality of Service in the network.\n\n2. **iNet Capacity**:\n   - Below the "iNet QoS Enforcer," there is a rounded rectangle labeled "iNet Capacity." This likely represents the total capacity or resources available in the network.\n\n3. **GSP-1, GSP-2, GSP-3**:\n   - From the "iNet Capacity," three branches extend to the left, labeled "GSP-1," "GSP-2," and "GSP-3." These likely represent different service providers or groups within the network.\n\n4. **GSP-1.1 and GSP-1.2**:\n   - From the "GSP-1" branch, two further branches extend downward, labeled "GSP-1.1" and "GSP-1.2." These could represent sub-groups or specific services under GSP-1.\n\n5. **Terminal VR Demand**:\n   - At the bottom of the diagram, there is a label "Terminal VR Demand," which appears to be a section or a group of terminals with varying demands for virtual resources.\n\n6. **Terminal VR Demand Breakdown**:\n   - Below the "Terminal VR Demand" label, there are five rectangular boxes, each labeled with a terminal identifier and a VR (Virtual Resource) identifier:\n     - Terminal-2-SSPC-1 VR-1\n     - Terminal-3-SSPC-1 VR-1\n     - Terminal-1-SSPC-1 VR-1\n     - Terminal-4-SSPC-2 VR-1\n     - Terminal-4-SSPC-2 VR-2\n\n7. **Connections**:\n   - The diagram shows connections between the "iNet Capacity" and the "GSP-1," "GSP-2," and "GSP-3" branches, indicating that these groups are connected to the overall network capacity.\n   - The "GSP-1" branch further connects to "GSP-1.1" and "GSP-1.2," and these branches connect to the "Terminal VR Demand" section, suggesting a hierarchical structure where the network capacity is allocated to different service providers and terminals.\n\nThis diagram seems to represent a hierarchical network architecture where the "iNet QoS Enforcer" manages the overall network capacity, which is then allocated to different service providers (GSPs) and further divided among specific terminals with varying VR demands.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(714.5833333333333), np.float64(1195.8333333333333)), (np.float64(714.5833333333333), np.float64(2475.520833333333)), (np.float64(2479.1666666666665), np.float64(2475.520833333333)), (np.float64(2479.1666666666665), np.float64(1195.8333333333333))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 199, 'image_path': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-199-123.jpg', 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'category': 'Image', 'element_id': '3649f09d42d098f37f4347f34463ea29', 'source_image': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-199-123.jpg'}, page_content='[IMAGE DESCRIPTION]\nSource File: /Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-199-123.jpg\nThe image is a flowchart illustrating the process of Quality of Service (QoS) enforcement across different levels of a network system. The flowchart is divided into four main sections, each representing a different component of the QoS system: Global QoS Enforcer, SAS QoS Enforcer, iNet QoS Enforcer, and Terminal Proxy. The flowchart includes arrows indicating the direction of data flow and text labels describing the nature of the data being exchanged.\n\n### Detailed Description:\n\n1. **Global QoS Enforcer:**\n   - Located at the far left.\n   - Sends data once every 5 seconds.\n   - Exchanges data with the SAS QoS Enforcer.\n   - Exchanges data with the iNet QoS Enforcer.\n   - Exchanges data with the Terminal Proxy.\n   - The data exchanged includes:\n     - Aggregate SAS Demand | Usage\n     - QoS Adjustment per SAS\n     - Aggregate iNet Demand | Usage\n     - QoS Adjustment per iNet\n\n2. **SAS QoS Enforcer:**\n   - Located in the middle left.\n   - Sends data once every 1 second.\n   - Exchanges data with the Global QoS Enforcer.\n   - Exchanges data with the iNet QoS Enforcer.\n   - Exchanges data with the Terminal Proxy.\n   - The data exchanged includes:\n     - Aggregate iNet Demand | Usage\n     - QoS Adjustment per iNet\n\n3. **iNet QoS Enforcer:**\n   - Located in the middle right.\n   - Sends data at a rate of 100/125 milliseconds.\n   - Exchanges data with the SAS QoS Enforcer.\n   - Exchanges data with the Terminal Proxy.\n   - The data exchanged includes:\n     - Demand\n     - Allocation\n     - Aggregate iNet Demand | Usage\n     - QoS Adjustment per iNet\n\n4. **Terminal Proxy:**\n   - Located on the far right.\n   - Sends data at a rate of 100/125 milliseconds.\n   - Exchanges data with the iNet QoS Enforcer.\n   - The data exchanged includes:\n     - Demand\n     - Allocation\n\n### Key Points:\n- The flowchart shows a hierarchical structure where data is aggregated and adjusted at each level before being sent to the next level.\n- The Global QoS Enforcer is the highest level, providing overall demand and usage data.\n- The Terminal Proxy is the lowest level, where individual demands and allocations are managed.\n- The QoS adjustments are made at each level to ensure that the network resources are allocated efficiently based on the demands and usages reported.\n\nThis flowchart is likely used in a network management system to ensure that QoS is maintained across different network segments and devices.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(568.75), np.float64(430.2083333333333)), (np.float64(568.75), np.float64(1782.8124999999998)), (np.float64(2541.145833333333), np.float64(1782.8124999999998)), (np.float64(2541.145833333333), np.float64(430.2083333333333))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 200, 'image_path': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-200-124.jpg', 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'category': 'Image', 'element_id': 'ed4ffbff38599d175549a87aec61a8fc', 'source_image': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-200-124.jpg'}, page_content='[IMAGE DESCRIPTION]\nSource File: /Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-200-124.jpg\nThe image is a detailed diagram illustrating a hierarchical structure of Quality of Service (QoS) management in a network environment. The diagram is divided into three main sections, each representing a different level of QoS enforcement and capacity management:\n\n1. **iNet QoS Enforcer**:\n   - This section is at the top left and is responsible for making adjustments at the iNet level. It adjusts the iNet Level Tree with 100/125 ms adjustments.\n   - It connects to the "iNet Capacity" section, which further divides into GSP-1, GSP-2, and GSP-3.\n   - The GSP-1 further divides into GSP-1.1 and GSP-1.2, which are connected to the "Terminal VR Demand" section. This section lists specific terminals: Terminal 1-2-SSPC-1 VR-1, Terminal 1-3-SSPC-1 VR-1, Terminal 1-1-SSPC-1 VR-1, Terminal 4-SSPC-2 VR-1, and Terminal 4-SSPC-2 VR-2.\n\n2. **SAS QoS Enforcer**:\n   - This section is at the top center and is responsible for making adjustments at the SAS level. It adjusts the SAS Level Tree with 1 sec. adjustments.\n   - It connects to the "SAS Capacity" section, which further divides into GSP-1, GSP-2, and GSP-3.\n   - The GSP-1 further divides into GSP-1.1 and GSP-1.2, which are connected to the "Aggregate iNet Demand" section. This section lists specific iNet nodes: iNet-1, iNet-2, and iNet-N.\n\n3. **Global QoS Enforcer**:\n   - This section is at the top right and is responsible for making adjustments at the global level. It adjusts the global level tree with 5 sec. adjustments.\n   - It connects to the "Global Capacity" section, which further divides into GSP-1, GSP-2, and GSP-3.\n   - The GSP-1 further divides into GSP-1.1 and GSP-1.2, which are connected to the "Aggregate SAS Demand" section. This section lists specific SAS nodes: SAS-1, SAS-2, and SAS-N.\n\nThe diagram also includes arrows indicating the flow of adjustments and capacity management from the QoS Enforcers to the respective capacity sections, and from the capacity sections to the demand sections. The overall structure is hierarchical, with the QoS Enforcers at the top, followed by capacity management, and finally, the demand sections at the bottom.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(794.7916666666666), np.float64(969.7916666666666)), (np.float64(794.7916666666666), np.float64(2085.4166666666665)), (np.float64(2395.3125), np.float64(2085.4166666666665)), (np.float64(2395.3125), np.float64(969.7916666666666))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 201, 'image_path': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-201-125.jpg', 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'category': 'Image', 'element_id': '09bfac37f8e3db0039c347e6282d82f0', 'source_image': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-201-125.jpg'}, page_content='[IMAGE DESCRIPTION]\nSource File: /Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-201-125.jpg\nThe image is a hierarchical diagram representing a network bandwidth allocation system. Here is a detailed description:\n\n1. **Top Level:**\n   - The topmost element is labeled "iNet Bandwidth Pool," which is the main resource pool from which bandwidth is allocated.\n\n2. **First Level:**\n   - The "iNet Bandwidth Pool" branches out into two main categories:\n     - **GSP-1**\n     - **GSP-2**\n     - **NMS GSP (Priority 1)**\n\n3. **Second Level:**\n   - **GSP-1** branches into three subcategories:\n     - GSP-11\n     - GSP-12\n     - GSP-13\n   - **GSP-2** branches into two subcategories:\n     - GSP-21\n     - GSP-22\n   - **NMS GSP (Priority 1)** is a separate category with no further subcategories.\n\n4. **Third Level:**\n   - Each of the GSP subcategories (GSP-11, GSP-12, GSP-13, GSP-21, GSP-22) branches into a single subcategory labeled "SSPC" (System Specific Partition Category).\n   - The "SSPC" categories are:\n     - SSPC-1\n     - SSPC-2\n     - SSPC-3\n   - The "NMS GSP (Priority 1)" branches into a single subcategory labeled "NMS SSPC" (NMS System Specific Partition Category).\n\n5. **Bottom Level:**\n   - Each "SSPC" category is associated with a "Terminal":\n     - SSPC-1 is associated with Terminal-1.\n     - SSPC-2 is associated with Terminal-2.\n     - SSPC-3 is associated with Terminal-3.\n   - The "NMS SSPC" is associated with the "NMS" (Network Management System).\n\n6. **Text at the Bottom:**\n   - The text at the bottom of the diagram states:\n     - "NMS partition at top level with the highest priority."\n     - "Each terminal has an NMS SSPC."\n\nThis diagram illustrates a structured approach to bandwidth allocation, with the NMS partition having the highest priority and each terminal having its own dedicated SSPC.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': (