figures/figure-225-153.jpg', 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'category': 'Image', 'element_id': '4120321988613e151eb88b86aed83c99', 'source_image': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-225-153.jpg'}, page_content='[IMAGE DESCRIPTION]\nSource File: /Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-225-153.jpg\nThe image is a diagram illustrating a network architecture, specifically a network management setup. Here is a detailed description:\n\n1. **Primary NOC (Network Operations Center)**:\n   - Located at the top left of the diagram.\n   - It is connected to the "Backbone Network" via a line.\n\n2. **Backbone Network**:\n   - A large cloud-like shape in the center of the diagram represents the backbone network.\n   - It connects to three primary sites labeled as "Primary SAS 1," "Primary SAS 2," and "Primary SAS 3."\n\n3. **Primary Sites (SAS)**:\n   - Each primary site is connected to the backbone network.\n   - The primary sites are labeled as "Primary SAS 1," "Primary SAS 2," and "Primary SAS 3."\n   - Each primary site has a corresponding backup/diversity site, labeled as "Backup/Diversity SAS 1," "Backup/Diversity SAS 2," and "Backup/Diversity SAS 3."\n\n4. **Backup/Diversity Sites**:\n   - These sites are connected to the backbone network but are not directly connected to the primary sites.\n   - They are positioned below the primary sites and are labeled as "Backup/Diversity SAS 1," "Backup/Diversity SAS 2," and "Backup/Diversity SAS 3."\n\n5. **Key Legend**:\n   - Located at the top right of the diagram.\n   - It indicates the status of the sites:\n     - A blue building icon with a checkmark is labeled "active."\n     - A gray building icon with a checkmark is labeled "standby."\n\n6. **Building Icons**:\n   - The blue building icons represent active sites.\n   - The gray building icons represent standby sites.\n\nThe diagram visually represents a network architecture where the primary sites are active and the backup/diversity sites are standby, ensuring redundancy and failover capabilities in the network.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(590.625), np.float64(1385.4166666666665)), (np.float64(590.625), np.float64(1910.4166666666665)), (np.float64(2603.125), np.float64(1910.4166666666665)), (np.float64(2603.125), np.float64(1385.4166666666665))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 226, 'image_path': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-226-154.jpg', 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'category': 'Image', 'element_id': 'd390c4760f1e0faddd0111abc1bbafbd', 'source_image': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-226-154.jpg'}, page_content='[IMAGE DESCRIPTION]\nSource File: /Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-226-154.jpg\nThe image is a diagram illustrating a network architecture, specifically a backbone network with a satellite connection to a terminal. Here is a detailed description of the components and their connections:\n\n1. **Backbone Network**:\n   - On the left side of the diagram, there is a section labeled "Backbone Network."\n\n2. **Active Primary SAS (System Access Switch)**:\n   - This is the main system access switch in the network.\n   - It is connected to the "Satellite" via a line labeled "Active Primary SAS."\n\n3. **Satellite**:\n   - The satellite is connected to the "Terminal" on the right side of the diagram.\n   - The connection from the satellite to the terminal is labeled "Satellite."\n\n4. **Terminal**:\n   - This is the end device or system that the satellite connects to.\n\n5. **Components in the Network**:\n   - **SAS NMS (System Access Switch Network Management System)**: This is a network management system for the SAS.\n   - **Access Router**: This is a router that connects the backbone network to the PPs (Point-to-Point) network.\n   - **PPs (Point-to-Point)**: These are point-to-point connections that carry data between the access router and the line cards.\n   - **Line Cards**: These are devices that handle the actual data transmission and routing.\n   - **RFS (Radio Frequency Switch)**: This is a switch that manages the radio frequency connections.\n   - **Standby Backup SAS**: This is a secondary system access switch that is in a standby mode, ready to take over in case of failure of the Active Primary SAS.\n\n6. **Connections**:\n   - The backbone network is connected to the access router.\n   - The access router is connected to the PPs.\n   - The PPs are connected to the line cards.\n   - The line cards are connected to the RFS.\n   - The RFS is connected to the Active Primary SAS.\n   - The Active Primary SAS is connected to the Satellite.\n   - The Satellite is connected to the Terminal.\n\nThe diagram shows a redundant network architecture where the Standby Backup SAS is in a standby mode, ready to take over if the Active Primary SAS fails. The entire setup ensures high availability and reliability in the network.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(590.625), np.float64(2176.5625)), (np.float64(590.625), np.float64(2351.5625)), (np.float64(765.6249999999999), np.float64(2351.5625)), (np.float64(765.6249999999999), np.float64(2176.5625))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modifi