', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 396, 'image_path': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-396-282.jpg', 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'category': 'Image', 'element_id': '4e8d97918ef0dc13cb826ad0068eb634', 'source_image': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-396-282.jpg'}, page_content='[IMAGE DESCRIPTION]\nSource File: /Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-396-282.jpg\nThe image is a detailed diagram illustrating the architecture of a Satellite Virtual Network (SVN). Here is a breakdown of the components and their relationships:\n\n### Title:\n- The title at the top of the image reads "Satellite Virtual Network (SVN)."\n\n### Directional Arrows:\n- There are two directional arrows at the top of the image. The blue arrow on the left points to the left and is labeled "IPVPN," indicating the direction of the Internet Protocol Virtual Private Network. The blue arrow on the right points to the right and is labeled "IP," indicating the direction of the Internet Protocol.\n- The orange arrow in the middle points to the left and is labeled "L2oS," which stands for Layer 2 over Satellite, indicating the direction of the Layer 2 service over the satellite network.\n\n### Components:\n1. **Left Side:**\n   - **Internet or Private Network:** This is the starting point of the network.\n   - **Backbone Network:** This is the network that connects various parts of the infrastructure.\n   - **Access Router:** This is a device that connects the backbone network to the satellite network.\n\n2. **Middle Section:**\n   - **SAS LAN (Satellite Access Subnet):** This is the satellite access subnet.\n   - **PP (Point-to-Point):** This likely stands for Point-to-Point, indicating a direct connection.\n   - **OTA Satellite Network:** This is the satellite network itself.\n   - **Terminal LAN:** This is the local area network at the terminal.\n   - **Terminal:** This is the device or system at the end of the network.\n\n3. **Right Side:**\n   - **Subnet:** This is the final destination of the network, which is a subnet of the local area network.\n\n### Connections:\n- The diagram shows the flow of data from the Internet or Private Network through the backbone network, access router, satellite access subnet, satellite network, terminal LAN, and finally to the subnet.\n- The L2oS service is highlighted in the middle, indicating that it is the service that connects the Ethernet and satellite components.\n\n### Labels:\n- Each component is labeled with its name, such as "Internet or Private Network," "Backbone Network," "Access Router," "SAS LAN," "OTA Satellite Network," "Terminal LAN," "Terminal," and "Subnet."\n\n### Overall:\n- The diagram is a comprehensive representation of how a satellite network connects to a private or public network, utilizing Layer 2 services over satellite to ensure secure and reliable communication.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(590.625), np.float64(1476.5624999999998)), (np.float64(590.625), np.float64(1651.5624999999998)), (np.float64(765.6249999999999), np.float64(1651.5624999999998)), (np.float64(765.6249999999999), np.float64(1476.5624999999998))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 396, 'image_path': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-396-283.jpg', 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'category': 'Image', 'element_id': '148c536274490eca3caf21d0d5b4375d', 'source_image': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-396-283.jpg'}, page_content='[IMAGE DESCRIPTION]\nSource File: /Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-396-283.jpg\nThe image appears to be a pixelated representation of a blue rectangular shape with a white interior. The edges of the rectangle are jagged, indicating that the image is low-resolution or has been pixelated. There is no text, numbers, charts, or diagrams visible within the rectangle. The overall appearance suggests that this might be a simplified or abstract representation of a shape, possibly a gift box or a similar object, given the shape and the way it is presented. The blue color could represent a ribbon or a bow, which is often associated with gift wrapping.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(590.625), np.float64(2326.0416666666665)), (np.float64(590.625), np.float64(2501.0416666666665)), (np.float64(765.6249999999999), np.float64(2501.0416666666665)), (np.float64(765.6249999999999), np.float64(2326.0416666666665))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 396, 'image_path': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-396-284.jpg', 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'category': 'Image', 'element_id': '36a81f247e62bb5f5e067a7b3d4fc48e', 'source_image': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-396-284.jpg'}, page_content='[IMAGE DESCRIPTION]\nSource File: /Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-396-284.jpg\nThe image appears to be a pixelated representation of a blue gift box with a white interior. The box is open, and there is a blue ribbon tied around it, with a small blue bow on top. The ribbon and bow are also pixelated, maintaining the same blue color scheme as the box. There is no text, numbers, charts, or diagrams visible in the image. The overall design is simple and minimalistic, with a focus on the blue color and the shape of the gift box.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(856.7708333333333), np.float64(652.6041666666666)), (np.float64(856.7708333333333), np.float64(1578.6458333333333)), (np.float64(2333.333333333333), np.float64(1578.6458333333333)), (np.float64(2333.333333333333), np.float64(652.6041666666666))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 398, 'image_path': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-398-285.jpg', 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'category': 'Image', 'element_id': '0c10cd95a13e675bfba1337ce8aee7e8', 'source_image': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-398-285.jpg'}, page_content='[IMAGE DESCRIPTION]\nSource File: /Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-398-285.jpg\nThe image illustrates the architecture of an Ethernet Virtual Connection (EVC) service, which is a type of service provided by a network service provider (PSN) to connect two customer edge devices (CE1 and CE2) across a network. The diagram is divided into several components and labeled with specific terms to explain the flow of data and the structure of the service.\n\n### Key Components and Labels:\n\n1. **CE1 (Customer Edge 1)**:\n   - This is the customer edge device on the left side of the diagram.\n   - It is connected to the network through an attachment circuit.\n\n2. **CE2 (Customer Edge 2)**:\n   - This is the customer edge device on the right side of the diagram.\n   - It is also connected to the network through an attachment circuit.\n\n3. **Attachment Circuit**:\n   - This is the connection point between the customer edge device and the provider edge device (PE1 and PE2).\n\n4. **PE1 (Provider Edge 1)**:\n   - This is the first provider edge device located between CE1 and PE2.\n   - It is responsible for terminating the attachment circuit from CE1 and forwarding the data to PE2.\n\n5. **PE2 (Provider Edge 2)**:\n   - This is the second provider edge device located between PE1 and CE2.\n   - It is responsible for receiving the data from PE1 and forwarding it to CE2.\n\n6. **PW1 (Provider-Wide 1)**:\n   - This is the first provider-wide connection that carries the data from PE1 to PE2.\n\n7. **PW2 (Provider-Wide 2)**:\n   - This is the second provider-wide connection that carries the data from PE2 back to PE1.\n\n8. **PSN Tunnel**:\n   - This is the tunnel that encapsulates the PW1 and PW2 connections, forming the PSN tunnel that connects CE1 to CE2.\n\n9. **Ethernet Virtual Connection**:\n   - This is the overall service that is being emulated, which connects CE1 to CE2 through the PSN tunnel.\n\n10. **Emulated Service**:\n    - This is the overall service that is being provided, which is the Ethernet Virtual Connection.\n\n### Flow of Data:\n\n- Data from CE1 is sent through the attachment circuit to PE1.\n- PE1 then forwards the data through PW1 to PE2.\n- PE2 receives the data from PW1 and forwards it through PW2 back to PE1.\n- Finally, the data is sent from PE1 through the attachment circuit to CE2.\n\n### Summary:\n\nThe diagram shows how an Ethernet Virtual Connection is established between two customer edge devices (CE1 and CE2) through a network service provider (PSN) using provider edge devices (PE1 and PE2) and provider-wide connections (PW1 and PW2). The PSN tunnel encapsulates these provider-wide connections to create the Ethernet Virtual Connection service.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(754.6874999999999), np.float64(525.0)), (np.float64(754.6874999999999), np.float64(1538.5416666666665)), (np.float64(2439.0625), np.float64(1538.5416666666665)), (np.float64(2439.0625), np.float64(525.0))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 399, 'image_path': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-399-286.jpg', 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'category': 'Image', 'element_id': '7c509b3b26cdc6fcd3ccf7c380a594e5', 'source_image': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-399-286.jpg'}, page_content='[IMAGE DESCRIPTION]\nSource File: /Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-399-286.jpg\nThe image illustrates a network architecture for an emulated L2 (Layer 2) service, which is a type of virtual private network (VPN) that emulates a Layer 2 network over a Layer 3 network. The diagram includes the following components and labels:\n\n1. **Customer Edge 1 (CE1)**:\n   - This is the edge device on the left side of the diagram.\n   - It is connected to the "Hub System" via an Ethernet interface with VLAN or QinQ tagging.\n\n2. **Hub System**:\n   - This is a central hub device that acts as a bridge between the customer edge devices.\n   - It is connected to the "Remote" device via a downstream link.\n\n3. **Remote**:\n   - This is the edge device on the right side of the diagram.\n   - It is connected to the "Hub System" via an upstream link.\n   - The Remote device is also connected to Customer Edge 2 (CE2) via an Ethernet interface with untagged VLAN or QinQ tagging.\n\n4. **Customer Edge 2 (CE2)**:\n   - This is the edge device on the right side of the diagram.\n   - It is connected to the Remote device via an Ethernet interface with untagged VLAN or QinQ tagging.\n\n5. **Emulated Connection**:\n   - This is the overall connection that is emulated over the Layer 3 network.\n   - It is shown as a dotted line connecting the Customer Edge 1 and Customer Edge 2.\n\n6. **SAT Interface**:\n   - This is the interface on the Hub System that connects to the downstream link.\n   - It is labeled as "SAT interface" and is part of the downstream connection.\n\n7. **Downstream**:\n   - This is the direction of data flow from the Hub System to the Remote device.\n   - It is shown as a blue arrow pointing from the Hub System to the Remote.\n\n8. **Upstream**:\n   - This is the direction of data flow from the Remote device to the Hub System.\n   - It is shown as a blue arrow pointing from the Remote to the Hub System.\n\n9. **Hub**:\n   - This is the central hub device that connects the Customer Edge 1 and Customer Edge 2.\n   - It is shown as a central node in the diagram.\n\n10. **Remote**:\n    - This is the edge device on the right side of the diagram.\n    - It is connected to the Hub System via an upstream link and to Customer Edge 2 via an Ethernet interface with untagged VLAN or QinQ tagging.\n\nThe diagram clearly illustrates the flow of data between the Customer Edge devices and the Hub System, showing the emulated Layer 2 service over a Layer 3 network. The use of VLAN or QinQ tagging on the Customer Edge devices and untagged VLAN or QinQ tagging on the Remote device highlights the tagging and untagging processes involved in the emulated L2 service.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(681.7708333333333), np.float64(1279.6875)), (np.float64(681.7708333333333), np.float64(1585.9374999999998)), (np.float64(2508.333333333333), np.float64(1585.9374999999998)), (np.float64(2508.333333333333), np.float64(1279.6875))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 400, 'image_path': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-400-287.jpg', 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'category': 'Image', 'element_id': '5438515158e3dba4241d4152336747ae', 'source_image': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-400-287.jpg'}, page_content='[IMAGE DESCRIPTION]\nSource File: /Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-400-287.jpg\nThe image is a diagram illustrating the structure of a data frame, specifically an Ethernet frame with VLAN tagging. Here is a detailed description:\n\n1. **MAC-dest (Destination MAC Address)**:\n   - Located at the beginning of the frame.\n   - Size: 6 bytes.\n   - Represents the destination MAC address of the frame.\n\n2. **MAC-src (Source MAC Address)**:\n   - Located immediately after the MAC-dest field.\n   - Size: 6 bytes.\n   - Represents the source MAC address of the frame.\n\n3. **SDT (Subtype Data Tag)**:\n   - Located after the MAC-src field.\n   - Size: 4 bytes.\n   - Consists of two parts:\n     - The first 2 bytes are fixed and have the value `0x8100`.\n     - The next 2 bytes are the VLAN Identifier (VID).\n\n4. **VID (VLAN Identifier)**:\n   - Located within the SDT field.\n   - Size: 2 bytes.\n   - Represents the VLAN Identifier, which is used to identify the VLAN to which the frame belongs.\n\n5. **Ether-type (Ethernet Type)**:\n   - Located after the VID field.\n   - Size: 2 bytes.\n   - Represents the type of the payload data. This field is used to identify the protocol encapsulated within the Ethernet frame.\n\n6. **Payload**:\n   - Located at the end of the frame.\n   - Size: Variable.\n   - Contains the actual data being transmitted, which can be any type of data depending on the Ether-type value.\n\nThe diagram is color-coded for clarity:\n- **MAC-dest**: Purple\n- **MAC-src**: Orange\n- **SDT**: Yellow\n- **VID**: Green\n- **Ether-type**: Green\n- **Payload**: Light green\n\nThe diagram is labeled with the sizes of each field in bytes (B) and the specific values or types of the fields.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(674.4791666666666), np.float64(1498.4374999999998)), (np.float64(674.4791666666666), np.float64(2125.520833333333)), (np.float64(2519.270833333333), np.float64(2125.520833333333)), (np.float64(2519.270833333333), np.float64(1498.4374999999998))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 401, 'image_path': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-401-288.jpg', 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'category': 'Image', 'element_id': '9348ead215146961ed1eb388dd453af9', 'source_image': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-401-288.jpg'}, page_content='[IMAGE DESCRIPTION]\nSource File: /Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-401-288.jpg\nThe image is a diagram illustrating a network topology, specifically focusing on a broadcast domain and its edges. Here is a detailed description:\n\n1. **Overall Structure**:\n   - The diagram is divided into two main sections: the left side and the right side.\n   - The left side is labeled as "Broadcast Domain Edge (typically router)" and shows a connection to a "SAS Switch."\n   - The right side is labeled as "Switch" and contains a "Satellite link And PP cluster" with three "Terminal" boxes stacked vertically.\n\n2. **Left Side**:\n   - A "Broadcast Domain Edge" is shown, which is typically a router.\n   - This router is connected to a "SAS Switch."\n\n3. **Right Side**:\n   - The "Switch" is the central hub of the network.\n   - Inside the "Switch," there is a "Satellite link And PP cluster."\n   - Below the "Satellite link And PP cluster," there are three "Terminal" boxes, each representing a terminal device or node.\n   - Each "Terminal" box is connected to the "Switch" via lines, indicating a connection.\n\n4. **Broadcast Domain Edges**:\n   - On the right side of the diagram, there are three "Broadcast domain edge" labels, each associated with a "Terminal" box.\n   - These labels indicate the boundaries of the broadcast domain, suggesting that each terminal is part of a separate broadcast domain.\n\n5. **Connections**:\n   - The "SAS Switch" on the left is connected to the "Switch" on the right.\n   - The "Switch" on the right is connected to the three "Terminal" boxes, which are also labeled as "Broadcast domain edge."\n\nThis diagram illustrates a network topology where the broadcast domain is segmented into smaller broadcast domains at the terminal level, with the SAS switch acting as a boundary between the broadcast domain edge and the satellite link cluster.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad90