MAGE DESCRIPTION]\nSource File: /Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-408-296.jpg\nThe image is a diagram illustrating a network topology with labeled components and arrows indicating the flow of data or connections. Here is a detailed description:\n\n1. **Components:**\n   - **CE-GW (Customer Edge Gateway):** This is a blue circular icon with a white symbol in the center, labeled as "CE-GW."\n   - **PE-Hub (Provider Edge Hub):** This is a gray rectangular icon labeled as "PE-Hub."\n   - **PE-Remote (Provider Edge Remote):** This is a gray rectangular icon labeled as "PE-Remote."\n   - **CE-RR (Customer Edge Remote):** This is a blue circular icon with a white symbol in the center, labeled as "CE-RR."\n\n2. **Arrows and Numbers:**\n   - **Number 1:** An arrow points from the CE-GW to the PE-Hub.\n   - **Number 2:** An arrow points from the PE-Remote to the CE-RR.\n   - **Number 3:** An arrow points from the PE-Hub to the PE-Remote.\n   - **Number 4:** An arrow points from the PE-Remote back to the PE-Hub.\n\n3. **Flow of Data:**\n   - The flow of data is indicated by the arrows and numbers. The data starts at the CE-GW and moves to the PE-Hub (number 1). It then moves from the PE-Hub to the PE-Remote (number 3), and from the PE-Remote to the CE-RR (number 2). Finally, the data loops back from the PE-Remote to the PE-Hub (number 4).\n\nThis diagram appears to represent a network topology where data is exchanged between different network devices, possibly in a hub-and-spoke or mesh network configuration.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(689.0625), np.float64(2490.1041666666665)), (np.float64(689.0625), np.float64(3069.7916666666665)), (np.float64(2501.0416666666665), np.float64(3069.7916666666665)), (np.float64(2501.0416666666665), np.float64(2490.1041666666665))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 410, 'image_path': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-410-297.jpg', 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'category': 'Image', 'element_id': '8d06bfb8030a1619db725703adccd722', 'source_image': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-410-297.jpg'}, page_content='[IMAGE DESCRIPTION]\nSource File: /Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-410-297.jpg\nThe image is a diagram illustrating a network architecture, specifically a Carrier Ethernet Network. Here is a detailed description of the components and elements in the image:\n\n1. **Customer Edge**:\n   - On the left side of the diagram, there is a building labeled "Customer Edge." This represents the edge of the customer\'s network where the connection to the Carrier Ethernet Network begins.\n   - There is a device labeled "UNI" (User-Network Interface) connected to the building. This interface is the point where the customer\'s network connects to the Carrier Ethernet Network.\n\n2. **Carrier Ethernet Network**:\n   - The central part of the diagram is labeled "Carrier Ethernet Network." This is represented by a cloud with a globe inside it, symbolizing the global reach of the network.\n   - The cloud is connected to multiple points, indicating that the network is interconnected and spans a wide area.\n\n3. **ISP POP (Internet Service Provider Point of Presence)**:\n   - On the right side of the diagram, there is another building labeled "ISP POP." This represents the location where the ISP (Internet Service Provider) has its network infrastructure.\n   - There is a device labeled "UNI" connected to the building, similar to the Customer Edge, indicating the point where the ISP\'s network connects to the Carrier Ethernet Network.\n\n4. **Internet**:\n   - The diagram shows a connection from the ISP POP to the Internet, represented by a globe with a network pattern around it. This indicates that the ISP POP is connected to the global Internet.\n\n5. **Storage Service Provider**:\n   - On the right side of the diagram, there is another building labeled "Storage Service Provider." This represents a third-party service provider that offers storage services.\n   - There is a device labeled "UNI" connected to the building, indicating the point where the Storage Service Provider\'s network connects to the Carrier Ethernet Network.\n\n6. **Connections**:\n   - The diagram shows multiple connections using lines labeled "UNI" (User-Network Interface). These lines represent the physical or logical connections between the Customer Edge, ISP POP, and Storage Service Provider and the Carrier Ethernet Network.\n\nOverall, the diagram illustrates a network architecture where a customer\'s network connects to a Carrier Ethernet Network, which in turn connects to an ISP POP and a Storage Service Provider, ultimately leading to the global Internet. The UNI points indicate the interfaces where these connections are made.'), Document(metadata={'source': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T/rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'coordinates': {'points': ((np.float64(889.5833333333333), np.float64(1028.125)), (np.float64(889.5833333333333), np.float64(1636.9791666666665)), (np.float64(2304.1666666666665), np.float64(1636.9791666666665)), (np.float64(2304.1666666666665), np.float64(1028.125))), 'system': 'PixelSpace', 'layout_width': 2975, 'layout_height': 3850}, 'last_modified': '2026-08-24T13:52:40', 'filetype': 'application/pdf', 'languages': ['eng'], 'page_number': 411, 'image_path': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-411-298.jpg', 'file_directory': '/var/folders/5z/p05w2vpj7b90clpntd1kvyp80000gp/T', 'filename': 'rag_upload_2390dad9058e9d21ffdcdecf211d325c.pdf', 'category': 'Image', 'element_id': 'a43c57e0f2225fff2880b3e8f2320ba3', 'source_image': '/Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-411-298.jpg'}, page_content='[IMAGE DESCRIPTION]\nSource File: /Users/prathod/Projects/Rag_based_q-a_boat_over_own_doc/src/q&a_boat/figures/figure-411-298.jpg\nThe image is a diagram illustrating a network setup involving a Point-to-Point EVC (Ethernet Virtual Connection) within a CEN (Core-Edge Network). Here is a detailed description:\n\n1. **Cloud Representation**: The central part of the diagram is represented by a cloud labeled "CEN," which stands for Core-Edge Network. This cloud symbolizes the core network infrastructure.\n\n2. **Point-to-Point EVC**: Inside the cloud, the text "Point-to-Point EVC" is written, indicating that the network setup involves a dedicated point-to-point Ethernet Virtual Connection.\n\n3. **CE Devices**: On either side of the cloud, there are two CE (Customer Edge) devices. These devices are connected to the cloud via a line labeled "Point-to-Point EVC."\n\n4. **Root UNI A and Root UNI B**: \n   - On the left side of the diagram, there is a CE device labeled "Root UNI A."\n   - On the right side of the diagram, there is another CE device labeled "Root UNI B."\n\n5. **Direction of Connection**: The line connecting the two CE devices is straight and horizontal, indicating a direct point-to-point connection through the CEN.\n\n6. **Text Labels**: \n   - "CE" is written on the CE devices, indicating that these are Customer Edge devices.\n   - "Root UNI A" and "Root UNI B" are labels for the respective CE devices, indicating their roles in the network.\n\nThe diagram is a simplified representation of a network topology where two CE devices are connected through a dedicated Ethernet Virtual Connection within a larger network infrastructure.'), Document(